WEBVTT

1
00:00:11,208 --> 00:00:14,500
[Slow, seductive double bass melody]

2
00:00:15,792 --> 00:00:19,167
[<i>Spooky </i>by Dusty Springfield plays]

3
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:24,750
♪ <i>In the cool of the evening
When everything is getting kind of groovy</i>

4
00:00:27,542 --> 00:00:29,250
♪ <i>You call me up and ask me</i>

5
00:00:29,333 --> 00:00:33,000
♪ <i>Would I like to go with you
And see a movie</i>

6
00:00:35,917 --> 00:00:39,583
♪ <i>But first I say no
I've got some plans for tonight</i>

7
00:00:39,667 --> 00:00:40,917
♪ <i>And then I stop</i>

8
00:00:42,167 --> 00:00:44,500
♪ <i>And say alright</i>

9
00:00:44,583 --> 00:00:48,792
♪ <i>Love is kind of crazy
With a spooky little boy like you</i>

10
00:00:52,667 --> 00:00:54,458
♪ <i>You always keep me guessing</i>

11
00:00:54,542 --> 00:00:57,917
♪ <i>I never seem to know
What you are thinking</i>

12
00:01:00,375 --> 00:01:03,250
♪ <i>And if a girl looks at you</i>

13
00:01:03,333 --> 00:01:06,208
♪ <i>It's for sure
Your little eye will be a winking</i>

14
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:11,000
♪ <i>I get confused</i>

15
00:01:11,083 --> 00:01:13,208
♪ <i>'Cause I don't know where I stand</i>

16
00:01:13,292 --> 00:01:15,000
♪ <i>And then you smile</i>

17
00:01:15,542 --> 00:01:17,875
♪ <i>And hold my hand</i>

18
00:01:17,958 --> 00:01:22,333
♪ <i>Love is kind of crazy
With a spooky little boy like you</i>

19
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:26,042
♪ <i>Spooky</i>

20
00:01:30,458 --> 00:01:35,500
♪ <i>If you decide someday to stop
This little game that you are playing</i>

21
00:01:38,750 --> 00:01:43,750
♪ <i>I'm gonna tell you all the things
My heart's dying to be saying</i>

22
00:01:47,083 --> 00:01:50,625
♪ <i>Just like a ghost you've been
A haunting my dreams</i>

23
00:01:50,708 --> 00:01:51,917
♪ <i>So now I know</i>

24
00:01:53,125 --> 00:01:55,417
♪ <i>You're not what you seem</i>

25
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:59,708
♪ <i>Love is kind of crazy
With a spooky little boy like you</i>

26
00:02:01,833 --> 00:02:03,917
♪ <i>Spooky</i>

27
00:02:05,958 --> 00:02:08,042
♪ <i>Spooky</i>

28
00:02:11,083 --> 00:02:14,375
♪ <i>Oh, oh, spooky</i>

29
00:02:15,042 --> 00:02:18,250
♪ <i>Alright, spooky </i>♪

30
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:21,708
[Music fades, resonates]

31
00:02:23,667 --> 00:02:26,083
[Delicate chimes]

32
00:02:28,667 --> 00:02:31,292
[Soft ambient music joins]

33
00:03:18,250 --> 00:03:20,708
[Music fades]

34
00:03:21,958 --> 00:03:23,875
[He groans]

35
00:03:33,667 --> 00:03:36,125
[He mutters to himself]

36
00:04:08,542 --> 00:04:11,208
So how does Daddy survive exactly?

37
00:04:11,708 --> 00:04:13,708
I don't think there's any social security

38
00:04:13,792 --> 00:04:17,250
'cause I don't really recall him
ever having a 'real' job.

39
00:04:17,333 --> 00:04:19,375
Yeah, I...

40
00:04:19,458 --> 00:04:21,458
I'm not completely sure.

41
00:04:22,167 --> 00:04:24,375
You know,
he always seems to have projects.

42
00:04:25,042 --> 00:04:26,333
[Woman] Uh-huh.

43
00:04:29,541 --> 00:04:31,916
He's gonna look old, isn't he?

44
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:33,750
Well, yeah, I guess.

45
00:04:33,833 --> 00:04:35,875
You said it's been two years?

46
00:04:35,958 --> 00:04:37,583
Give or take, yeah.

47
00:04:39,417 --> 00:04:41,667
I remember you
flew back here to see him.

48
00:04:41,750 --> 00:04:43,500
[Woman] We met at a restaurant.

49
00:04:43,583 --> 00:04:47,125
He said his place wasn't, uh, presentable.

50
00:04:47,208 --> 00:04:49,083
[Man] Well, yeah. It probably wasn't.

51
00:04:56,708 --> 00:04:58,542
[Woman] I still think about mom's funeral.

52
00:04:58,625 --> 00:05:00,042
That wasn't good.

53
00:05:00,542 --> 00:05:02,542
He was very upset though.

54
00:05:13,917 --> 00:05:15,833
Hmm.

55
00:05:23,083 --> 00:05:27,208
The last time he called me,
well, the last times he called me

56
00:05:27,292 --> 00:05:29,375
you know, he puts on a little show.

57
00:05:29,458 --> 00:05:31,333
The 'Dad' act, but...

58
00:05:31,417 --> 00:05:33,667
you know in the end,
it's always about money.

59
00:05:33,750 --> 00:05:35,417
Did you give him any?

60
00:05:35,500 --> 00:05:36,875
[Woman] No.

61
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:40,167
Well, a few years ago I did.

62
00:05:40,750 --> 00:05:43,083
Richard was furious
when he found out I'd sent it.

63
00:05:43,167 --> 00:05:44,833
But after that, no way.

64
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:48,000
You?

65
00:05:48,083 --> 00:05:50,667
Well, yeah. Sure, I've...

66
00:05:50,750 --> 00:05:52,542
I helped him out here and there.

67
00:05:54,917 --> 00:05:56,917
Man, Sheryl didn't like it either.

68
00:05:58,375 --> 00:06:00,542
[Woman] It's probably
why she divorced you.

69
00:06:04,333 --> 00:06:06,375
I'm sorry. I didn't--
I shouldn't have said that.

70
00:06:06,458 --> 00:06:08,875
It's okay.
There were other reasons.

71
00:06:10,750 --> 00:06:12,583
I'm sorry, Jeff.

72
00:06:17,833 --> 00:06:20,250
You see this?

73
00:06:20,333 --> 00:06:23,042
[Dreamy ambient music]

74
00:06:25,875 --> 00:06:28,292
Man, those skater kids are everywhere.

75
00:06:35,458 --> 00:06:37,708
They grow like weeds, right?

76
00:06:47,250 --> 00:06:49,167
[Woman] My Louis is a skater.

77
00:06:50,708 --> 00:06:52,917
[Music fades]

78
00:06:53,542 --> 00:06:55,250
He's the anarchist of the family.

79
00:06:55,333 --> 00:06:57,375
[Jeff chuckles]

80
00:06:57,458 --> 00:06:59,000
How old is he now? Twelve?

81
00:06:59,083 --> 00:07:00,625
Thirteen.

82
00:07:15,958 --> 00:07:18,375
[He mutters to himself]

83
00:07:18,458 --> 00:07:21,083
[Rapid footsteps]

84
00:07:21,167 --> 00:07:23,792
[Soft clattering]

85
00:07:31,333 --> 00:07:36,333
I did feel I needed to help him out
for those few, you know, emergencies.

86
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:39,417
What are you talking about?

87
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:41,500
The well, and the septic disaster.

88
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:43,417
You knew about that, right?

89
00:07:43,917 --> 00:07:44,917
No.

90
00:07:47,583 --> 00:07:49,792
Well, he really needed help, so...

91
00:07:52,083 --> 00:07:53,500
And then the wall thing.

92
00:07:53,583 --> 00:07:55,167
The wall thing?

93
00:07:55,250 --> 00:07:56,542
[Jeff] Yeah.

94
00:07:56,625 --> 00:07:58,208
This wall collapsed,

95
00:07:58,292 --> 00:08:01,167
and part of his house apparently caved in.

96
00:08:01,250 --> 00:08:02,667
Or almost did.

97
00:08:04,083 --> 00:08:06,375
Come on, Emmy.
He called you about that, no?

98
00:08:07,958 --> 00:08:08,958
No.

99
00:08:09,042 --> 00:08:11,292
Huh. Wow.

100
00:08:14,125 --> 00:08:16,000
Man, that was expensive.

101
00:08:23,125 --> 00:08:26,125
He told me recently
he can barely pay for electricity.

102
00:08:27,417 --> 00:08:29,042
Or the phone bill.

103
00:08:30,083 --> 00:08:31,917
His landline?

104
00:08:36,250 --> 00:08:37,958
Yeah, his landline.

105
00:08:45,707 --> 00:08:48,500
Dad's place is just over
this next hill, I think.

106
00:08:48,582 --> 00:08:50,082
Or maybe the next one.

107
00:08:59,292 --> 00:09:00,500
Okay.

108
00:09:04,167 --> 00:09:06,458
[Car indicator ticks]

109
00:09:13,833 --> 00:09:15,458
[Emily] Nowheresville.

110
00:09:26,417 --> 00:09:29,625
Should probably get this driveway graded.

111
00:09:57,583 --> 00:09:58,750
[Emily] Okay.

112
00:10:18,500 --> 00:10:21,083
[Birds call]

113
00:10:31,708 --> 00:10:33,292
I brought a few things for him.

114
00:10:33,375 --> 00:10:34,792
Of course you did.

115
00:10:36,250 --> 00:10:38,083
That's not looking too good.

116
00:11:10,250 --> 00:11:12,958
[Birds call in background]

117
00:11:25,292 --> 00:11:27,250
- [Father] Hey!
- [Emily] Oh, hi, Daddy.

118
00:11:27,333 --> 00:11:30,333
- [Jeff] Hi, Dad, it's us.
- Hey, Emily. Come on in, Jeff.

119
00:11:30,417 --> 00:11:33,375
- [Father mutters to himself]
- Come on in.

120
00:11:33,458 --> 00:11:34,667
Yeah.

121
00:11:35,417 --> 00:11:37,083
- Come on in.
- [Jeff] Excuse me.

122
00:11:37,167 --> 00:11:38,417
[Father] Okay.

123
00:11:40,042 --> 00:11:42,125
[Father mutters to himself]

124
00:11:42,917 --> 00:11:47,000
Okay, well, you look fantastic.

125
00:11:48,042 --> 00:11:50,542
And you don't look any older.

126
00:11:50,625 --> 00:11:52,875
That's debatable, but thank you.

127
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:56,417
Hey, that's a really great color on you.

128
00:11:56,500 --> 00:11:58,333
Oh, thanks.

129
00:11:58,417 --> 00:12:00,042
And on you, too.

130
00:12:00,125 --> 00:12:03,000
Oh, what a nice sweater, Jeff.

131
00:12:03,083 --> 00:12:04,708
Oh, look at that.

132
00:12:04,792 --> 00:12:06,333
- Oh, yeah.
- Yeah.

133
00:12:06,417 --> 00:12:08,250
I think it's a family thing.

134
00:12:08,333 --> 00:12:10,458
[Jeff and Emily chuckle softly]

135
00:12:10,542 --> 00:12:11,583
Yeah.

136
00:12:13,083 --> 00:12:15,250
Alright, so here we go.

137
00:12:15,333 --> 00:12:17,208
- [Emily] Sorry.
- Oh, sorry.

138
00:12:18,042 --> 00:12:20,917
Well, it's good to see you, son.

139
00:12:21,792 --> 00:12:23,792
You've always been my favourite son.

140
00:12:24,375 --> 00:12:26,125
Well, your only son.

141
00:12:26,208 --> 00:12:27,583
As far as we know.

142
00:12:28,167 --> 00:12:31,375
There's that same
sense of humor, huh?

143
00:12:32,458 --> 00:12:33,917
Come on inside.

144
00:12:34,500 --> 00:12:35,750
Okay.

145
00:12:37,125 --> 00:12:38,375
[Jeff] Oh.

146
00:12:39,125 --> 00:12:40,583
Yeah.

147
00:12:40,667 --> 00:12:41,958
Welcome.

148
00:12:43,292 --> 00:12:45,958
Oh, I like this chair.

149
00:12:46,042 --> 00:12:49,708
Oh, oh, yeah.
That, that's a special chair.

150
00:12:49,792 --> 00:12:52,792
Friend of mine made that and, uh...

151
00:12:54,375 --> 00:12:58,917
It's nice 'cause it swivels
and you look out the window.

152
00:12:59,542 --> 00:13:02,250
[Father mutters to himself]

153
00:13:05,208 --> 00:13:08,250
- [Emily] Oh, this is really nice, Daddy!
- [Father] Isn't it?

154
00:13:08,333 --> 00:13:10,417
- [Emily] So peaceful.
- [Father] Yeah.

155
00:13:12,583 --> 00:13:13,625
Uh...

156
00:13:13,708 --> 00:13:15,875
[He chuckles] What is this?

157
00:13:15,958 --> 00:13:18,292
These are just some things
I brought for you.

158
00:13:18,375 --> 00:13:20,458
Oh, wow. Oh, gosh!

159
00:13:20,542 --> 00:13:25,333
Wow, thanks.
I... um, looks like real,

160
00:13:25,417 --> 00:13:26,958
real quality here.

161
00:13:27,042 --> 00:13:29,333
[Father clears his throat, grumbles]

162
00:13:33,500 --> 00:13:36,708
Well, have a seat, darling.

163
00:13:37,583 --> 00:13:39,500
Make yourselves at home.

164
00:13:39,583 --> 00:13:41,583
- Thanks, Dad.
- Thank you.

165
00:13:43,125 --> 00:13:45,667
Hey, your furniture is kinda stylish.

166
00:13:45,750 --> 00:13:46,917
I like it.

167
00:13:48,250 --> 00:13:51,292
- Yeah, it's old.
- [Father chuckles]

168
00:13:51,375 --> 00:13:53,167
- Really?
- Yeah.

169
00:13:53,250 --> 00:13:54,875
It's still in pretty good shape.

170
00:13:54,958 --> 00:13:56,583
[Father] Okay. Thanks.

171
00:13:57,333 --> 00:13:58,792
Uh...

172
00:14:01,292 --> 00:14:04,500
I want you guys to know
that I'm so glad you're here.

173
00:14:04,583 --> 00:14:06,208
It really means a lot to me.

174
00:14:06,292 --> 00:14:08,250
And it's so great to see you both.

175
00:14:09,625 --> 00:14:12,833
And, and I know it's been a while.

176
00:14:16,125 --> 00:14:18,167
Can I get you anything?

177
00:14:18,250 --> 00:14:20,417
Some, how about some water or...

178
00:14:20,500 --> 00:14:23,250
Oh, is it your, your well water?

179
00:14:24,042 --> 00:14:25,417
Is the pump and all that fixed?

180
00:14:25,500 --> 00:14:28,833
Yeah. All working now, you know.

181
00:14:30,042 --> 00:14:32,292
All working thanks to you.

182
00:14:32,917 --> 00:14:34,500
Thank you, son.

183
00:14:34,583 --> 00:14:36,667
Let's get a little water going.

184
00:14:38,833 --> 00:14:41,333
[Metal squeaks;
Water gushes]

185
00:14:46,583 --> 00:14:48,958
What about ice?

186
00:14:49,042 --> 00:14:51,042
I think I got ice around here.

187
00:14:51,542 --> 00:14:53,167
Oh, no. Thanks though.

188
00:14:53,250 --> 00:14:56,375
- I'd like some ice.
- [Father] Oh, good. Ice coming up.

189
00:14:56,958 --> 00:15:00,208
I wish I had some lemon
or something to go with it.

190
00:15:04,167 --> 00:15:05,708
Okay.

191
00:15:06,417 --> 00:15:08,083
Here comes the water.

192
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,208
Oh, oh. Thanks, son.

193
00:15:15,667 --> 00:15:17,667
- Thank you.
- Yeah.

194
00:15:18,792 --> 00:15:20,792
- Thank you.
- Yeah.

195
00:15:25,083 --> 00:15:26,833
Well...

196
00:15:29,458 --> 00:15:31,875
Let's have a toast to, um...

197
00:15:33,167 --> 00:15:34,542
Um...

198
00:15:38,917 --> 00:15:40,583
To your wonderful mother.

199
00:15:42,333 --> 00:15:44,958
To... to our wonderful mother.

200
00:15:45,042 --> 00:15:47,458
- I'll drink to that. To mom.
- [Father] Yeah.

201
00:15:55,750 --> 00:15:59,000
[Father swallows audibly, exhales]

202
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,000
Is it okay to toast with water though?

203
00:16:05,083 --> 00:16:07,625
Oh, gosh. I... I think so.

204
00:16:07,708 --> 00:16:09,542
It's the purest drink.

205
00:16:09,625 --> 00:16:11,625
[He grumbles]

206
00:16:12,208 --> 00:16:13,833
And your mother loved water.

207
00:16:17,625 --> 00:16:20,708
And don't forget
she was a water sign, you know.

208
00:16:24,042 --> 00:16:25,292
Aquarius is a water sign?

209
00:16:25,375 --> 00:16:27,375
- Yeah. Aquarius is a water sign.
- [Father] Yeah.

210
00:16:27,458 --> 00:16:28,667
Ah.

211
00:16:29,375 --> 00:16:32,375
Not a water sign,
but it's a water carrier.

212
00:16:35,125 --> 00:16:37,625
[Water drips in the background]

213
00:16:39,833 --> 00:16:41,833
Do you hear that dripping?

214
00:16:41,917 --> 00:16:43,500
What is that?

215
00:16:43,583 --> 00:16:45,583
Oh, it's the...

216
00:16:46,417 --> 00:16:49,292
It's the kitchen faucet.

217
00:16:50,542 --> 00:16:54,333
You can't seem
to shut it off tight enough, and...

218
00:16:57,292 --> 00:16:59,458
But it does go away after a while.

219
00:17:00,625 --> 00:17:03,625
[Water continues dripping]

220
00:17:05,333 --> 00:17:07,208
[Father sighs]

221
00:17:39,083 --> 00:17:42,917
Wow. Wilhelm Reich,
'The Mass Psychology of Fascism'.

222
00:17:44,042 --> 00:17:46,750
I didn't realize you read
this kind of thing, Daddy.

223
00:17:46,833 --> 00:17:49,542
Uh... I had interests.

224
00:17:51,250 --> 00:17:53,083
It's been a while.

225
00:17:53,167 --> 00:17:54,792
[Emily] Noam Chomsky.

226
00:17:54,875 --> 00:17:55,958
Oh!

227
00:17:56,708 --> 00:17:59,083
Diogenes? Interesting.

228
00:18:00,125 --> 00:18:02,167
Father of cynicism.

229
00:18:02,750 --> 00:18:03,958
[She chuckles]

230
00:18:04,042 --> 00:18:05,167
That's appropriate.

231
00:18:07,625 --> 00:18:12,000
How are things back home, Em?
How are the kids?

232
00:18:12,583 --> 00:18:14,667
Both are doing well.

233
00:18:14,750 --> 00:18:17,583
Jennifer is a junior in high school.

234
00:18:17,667 --> 00:18:20,375
She's very smart and confident, and...

235
00:18:21,083 --> 00:18:22,750
Louis is in middle school.

236
00:18:22,833 --> 00:18:25,625
He's in a bit of
an awkward phase, but...

237
00:18:26,125 --> 00:18:27,792
he's finding himself.

238
00:18:27,875 --> 00:18:30,833
[Father] And... what about Richard?

239
00:18:30,917 --> 00:18:32,667
Fine. Solid as a rock.

240
00:18:32,750 --> 00:18:34,667
- [Father] Alright.
- As always.

241
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:40,958
Uh, Jeff, how's Sheryl?

242
00:18:41,042 --> 00:18:42,625
[She clears her throat]

243
00:18:43,333 --> 00:18:44,333
Uh...

244
00:18:45,167 --> 00:18:49,667
I mean, do you still
even stay in touch, or...

245
00:18:51,667 --> 00:18:53,458
No, Dad, we don't.

246
00:18:53,542 --> 00:18:58,958
Oh, well, that's tough, man.
I'm, I'm really sorry.

247
00:19:01,708 --> 00:19:03,792
What about work?
Is work going well?

248
00:19:03,875 --> 00:19:05,792
Yes. Yeah, thanks.

249
00:19:05,875 --> 00:19:10,167
I got, I...
I got that promotion I told you about.

250
00:19:10,250 --> 00:19:12,958
And I... and yeah.

251
00:19:13,042 --> 00:19:14,167
Working away.

252
00:19:14,250 --> 00:19:17,125
Okay, well, hey,
I'm really proud of you, son.

253
00:19:17,208 --> 00:19:19,500
I mean, you've really done
well for yourself.

254
00:19:19,583 --> 00:19:22,583
Well, thanks, Dad.

255
00:19:32,375 --> 00:19:34,958
[Soft, pensive music]

256
00:19:45,667 --> 00:19:47,500
Peaceful view, Dad.

257
00:19:49,125 --> 00:19:51,875
With the pond, the trees,

258
00:19:52,708 --> 00:19:53,875
and the horizon.

259
00:19:56,667 --> 00:19:58,500
So quiet here.

260
00:19:59,792 --> 00:20:01,000
[Father] Yeah.

261
00:20:02,375 --> 00:20:07,708
It's a step away
from the so-called 'real world'.

262
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:23,000
[Music fades]

263
00:20:27,458 --> 00:20:29,167
I made some tea.

264
00:20:29,250 --> 00:20:32,083
[Jeff] Oh. Tea.

265
00:20:32,167 --> 00:20:34,667
Yes. I found some
in the back of the cupboard.

266
00:20:34,750 --> 00:20:36,750
[Jeff] Thanks, Em.
I could use some tea.

267
00:20:37,708 --> 00:20:39,375
I didn't even know we had tea.

268
00:20:39,458 --> 00:20:40,917
[Emily chuckles]

269
00:20:47,292 --> 00:20:49,625
Oh! That's a nice watch, Daddy.

270
00:20:49,708 --> 00:20:51,333
Is that a Rolex?

271
00:20:52,625 --> 00:20:54,292
[Father] No, no.

272
00:20:54,375 --> 00:20:58,167
Yeah, I mean it's a... it's a fugazi.

273
00:20:58,708 --> 00:20:59,958
A Chinese replica.

274
00:21:00,542 --> 00:21:02,625
I liked the style.

275
00:21:03,750 --> 00:21:06,750
Huh. Well, looks real nice.

276
00:21:06,833 --> 00:21:08,167
[He sighs] Yeah.

277
00:21:12,333 --> 00:21:13,375
Hm.

278
00:21:13,458 --> 00:21:14,958
What?

279
00:21:16,417 --> 00:21:17,542
Um.

280
00:21:20,250 --> 00:21:24,875
What do you say we... toast our tea to...

281
00:21:25,583 --> 00:21:28,750
uh... family relations.

282
00:21:29,875 --> 00:21:32,042
[She laughs] Oh, okay.

283
00:21:32,125 --> 00:21:34,542
[Emily] Um, to family relations.

284
00:21:35,250 --> 00:21:36,583
To family, then.

285
00:21:36,667 --> 00:21:37,958
- To family.
- [Emily] To family.

286
00:21:38,042 --> 00:21:39,875
[Emily chuckles softly]

287
00:21:41,583 --> 00:21:43,708
[Father chuckles softly]

288
00:21:45,208 --> 00:21:47,792
- Hmm.
- Hm. Good?

289
00:21:47,875 --> 00:21:49,458
Excellent. Yeah.

290
00:21:52,583 --> 00:21:54,417
[Bird calls in the distance]

291
00:21:54,917 --> 00:21:57,417
Can you toast with tea though?

292
00:21:58,458 --> 00:22:00,542
- Well.
- Really?

293
00:22:00,625 --> 00:22:02,750
[Father chuckles softly]

294
00:22:05,333 --> 00:22:07,833
- [Father sighs]
- [She slurps tea softly]

295
00:22:11,333 --> 00:22:13,708
[He swallows, audibly]

296
00:22:24,083 --> 00:22:26,583
[Soft, pensive music]

297
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,542
[Jeff] I just wanna show you
some things I got for you.

298
00:22:52,625 --> 00:22:53,750
[Father] Oh. Wow.

299
00:22:53,833 --> 00:22:56,500
These are a few packages of pasta.

300
00:22:56,583 --> 00:22:58,500
- Very good ones.
- Oh, yeah.

301
00:22:58,583 --> 00:23:00,208
[Jeff] Italian.

302
00:23:00,292 --> 00:23:04,083
And some marinara sauce
with the cheese already in there.

303
00:23:04,167 --> 00:23:06,292
Oh, it's so much easier for me.

304
00:23:06,375 --> 00:23:08,208
- Thank you.
- And crackers, cookies.

305
00:23:08,292 --> 00:23:11,083
- Yeah, oh, yeah, cookies.
- Jar of dill pickle spears.

306
00:23:11,167 --> 00:23:12,583
Yeah, oh!

307
00:23:12,667 --> 00:23:14,458
- Canned tuna,
- Oh, man.

308
00:23:14,542 --> 00:23:16,375
...and then that French mustard you like.

309
00:23:16,458 --> 00:23:18,583
- [Father] Oh, thanks for remembering.
- [Jeff] Good stuff.

310
00:23:18,667 --> 00:23:19,667
[Father] Yeah, yeah.

311
00:23:19,750 --> 00:23:22,750
I got some fruit.
Apples, a few oranges.

312
00:23:22,833 --> 00:23:25,833
- That's the good stuff.
- Yes. Got some of this.

313
00:23:26,542 --> 00:23:27,917
Heaven's Door!

314
00:23:28,875 --> 00:23:31,333
Mezcal, too.
And some apple juice.

315
00:23:31,417 --> 00:23:33,917
Okay. Thank you. Thank you.

316
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,583
There's some dried ramen noodles
in there too.

317
00:23:36,667 --> 00:23:38,542
Oh, man. You got everything.

318
00:23:38,625 --> 00:23:39,667
Thank you!

319
00:23:39,750 --> 00:23:42,500
- Much appreciated, son.
- [Jeff] No problem.

320
00:23:42,583 --> 00:23:45,125
Yeah. And thank you, too, Em.

321
00:23:46,042 --> 00:23:48,208
Oh no, that's all from Jeff.

322
00:23:48,958 --> 00:23:51,833
- [Father] Oh, well.
- [Emily] So kind of him.

323
00:23:53,083 --> 00:23:55,292
[Father] Yeah. Yeah.

324
00:23:56,625 --> 00:24:00,250
It's wild because you're planning
on going into town,

325
00:24:00,333 --> 00:24:02,125
doing a grocery shop,

326
00:24:02,208 --> 00:24:05,167
you know, and then your kids show up
on the front porch,

327
00:24:05,250 --> 00:24:08,250
and I get this box
with all this great stuff in it,

328
00:24:08,333 --> 00:24:09,500
and Bob's your uncle.

329
00:24:12,708 --> 00:24:13,750
Um...

330
00:24:14,500 --> 00:24:17,167
English expression.
"Bob's your uncle".

331
00:24:19,208 --> 00:24:20,750
But Ted's our uncle.

332
00:24:22,333 --> 00:24:23,458
[Father] Never mind.

333
00:24:23,542 --> 00:24:25,667
[Father laughs]

334
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:31,083
Hey, Dad, do you want me
to take a look back

335
00:24:31,167 --> 00:24:32,875
where the wall was rebuilt last fall?

336
00:24:32,958 --> 00:24:35,958
No, no. It's... it's fine.

337
00:24:36,042 --> 00:24:38,042
It's probably pretty muddy
and snowy back there.

338
00:24:38,125 --> 00:24:40,500
I don't care about the mud.

339
00:24:40,583 --> 00:24:44,083
I don't really want you
to deal with it right now.

340
00:24:44,167 --> 00:24:47,375
I'm... trying to...

341
00:24:48,542 --> 00:24:49,750
Trying to what?

342
00:24:49,833 --> 00:24:52,125
I'm trying to... uh...

343
00:24:53,417 --> 00:24:55,917
well, there's a busted pipe, and...

344
00:24:58,250 --> 00:25:00,667
Ah, well, let me just take
a quick look then.

345
00:25:00,750 --> 00:25:02,583
No, no, son!

346
00:25:02,667 --> 00:25:05,625
No! I don't want
to deal with it right now.

347
00:25:05,708 --> 00:25:08,917
I just want to be together
and have this time...

348
00:25:10,167 --> 00:25:12,375
to spend together.

349
00:25:13,042 --> 00:25:15,208
I just don't want to have to...

350
00:25:15,708 --> 00:25:17,792
- worry about it right now.
- Okay, Dad.

351
00:25:19,750 --> 00:25:21,083
Yeah.

352
00:25:22,167 --> 00:25:25,250
Are you gonna be okay with heating fuel
for the rest of the winter?

353
00:25:25,333 --> 00:25:27,500
Prices are outrageous.

354
00:25:28,458 --> 00:25:30,875
Oh, yeah. I know they are.

355
00:25:30,958 --> 00:25:35,958
But I've got
this beautiful wood stove here.

356
00:25:36,750 --> 00:25:38,250
I chop my own wood.

357
00:25:38,333 --> 00:25:41,333
I'm like a real country bumpkin here.

358
00:25:41,417 --> 00:25:43,917
- [Emily chuckles, clears her throat]
- You know, I got the...

359
00:25:44,792 --> 00:25:46,542
I...

360
00:25:46,625 --> 00:25:48,833
I know what I'm doing around that.

361
00:25:48,917 --> 00:25:51,292
It's good and therapeutic for me

362
00:25:51,375 --> 00:25:53,125
'cause I...

363
00:25:53,208 --> 00:25:56,708
there's nothing
that feels as good as chopping.

364
00:25:56,792 --> 00:25:58,250
You know what I mean?

365
00:25:58,333 --> 00:26:04,750
When I'm chopping, I'm on it,
chopping and focused and chopping.

366
00:26:04,833 --> 00:26:08,292
I-I-I just, I don't know.

367
00:26:09,292 --> 00:26:13,625
I chop!
And I chop! And I chop!

368
00:26:13,708 --> 00:26:14,750
Whoa, okay, whoa!

369
00:26:14,833 --> 00:26:15,958
Oh, god, I...

370
00:26:16,042 --> 00:26:17,667
You're scaring me.

371
00:26:17,750 --> 00:26:20,417
I... That, that was wrong.

372
00:26:20,500 --> 00:26:22,208
I... I'm sorry.

373
00:26:23,042 --> 00:26:24,792
Sorry, honey. I got carried away.

374
00:26:25,417 --> 00:26:28,625
Sorry, sorry. I'm...

375
00:26:29,750 --> 00:26:31,333
Yeah. Mm-hmm.

376
00:26:33,667 --> 00:26:35,333
[He groans, softly]

377
00:26:43,208 --> 00:26:45,167
Daddy, how's, um...

378
00:26:45,708 --> 00:26:50,042
how's your health in general these days?

379
00:26:50,125 --> 00:26:52,083
Everything okay?

380
00:26:52,167 --> 00:26:54,583
Yeah. Yeah, I'm good.

381
00:26:54,667 --> 00:26:56,667
I'm good, in general.

382
00:26:58,917 --> 00:27:01,500
- [Soft, uneasy music]
- Any, any recent episodes?

383
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:05,833
- [Father] What do you mean?
- Uh...

384
00:27:05,917 --> 00:27:09,708
Like... you know,
like at mom's funeral.

385
00:27:11,958 --> 00:27:14,333
- [Music increases]
- [He sighs]

386
00:27:15,250 --> 00:27:18,167
Yeah. That, that was some time ago.

387
00:27:18,250 --> 00:27:23,833
But that, that was a particular... thing.

388
00:27:25,792 --> 00:27:27,625
Particular thing, yeah.

389
00:27:29,083 --> 00:27:31,792
Uhh, very emotional.

390
00:27:32,417 --> 00:27:33,417
Yeah.

391
00:27:33,500 --> 00:27:35,125
Yeah, for us all.

392
00:27:40,750 --> 00:27:43,667
[Father] Yeah, well, you two...

393
00:27:45,208 --> 00:27:47,583
handled it admirably.

394
00:27:50,208 --> 00:27:52,292
[Music concludes]

395
00:27:53,500 --> 00:27:56,583
May I ask if you still take anything?

396
00:27:57,333 --> 00:27:59,125
Take anything?

397
00:27:59,208 --> 00:28:00,292
Well,

398
00:28:01,292 --> 00:28:03,542
I take my coffee in the morning.

399
00:28:04,542 --> 00:28:05,833
Yeah.

400
00:28:06,917 --> 00:28:09,167
I mean like any medication.

401
00:28:11,125 --> 00:28:13,542
Well, no. No. No.

402
00:28:14,792 --> 00:28:16,583
[He mumbles]

403
00:28:16,667 --> 00:28:18,708
No, nothing like that. No. Really.

404
00:28:18,792 --> 00:28:20,167
Nothing like that.

405
00:28:22,500 --> 00:28:25,167
If, if that's what you're asking me,

406
00:28:25,250 --> 00:28:26,917
I-I don't...

407
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:29,000
No, I don't, I don't take drugs.

408
00:28:29,083 --> 00:28:32,875
I do have a drink now and then.

409
00:28:32,958 --> 00:28:35,125
But, no. Nothing. No, no...

410
00:28:36,250 --> 00:28:39,250
no heroin, no cocaine,

411
00:28:39,333 --> 00:28:42,667
no fentanyl, no oxycontin.

412
00:28:42,750 --> 00:28:44,708
No LSD,

413
00:28:44,792 --> 00:28:48,000
no marijuana.
No uppers, no downers.

414
00:28:48,083 --> 00:28:52,875
No goofballs, no... toad skins,

415
00:28:52,958 --> 00:28:56,042
no... and you know,

416
00:28:56,750 --> 00:28:58,208
no horse tranquilizers.

417
00:28:58,292 --> 00:29:00,583
Okay. Daddy. I just...

418
00:29:00,667 --> 00:29:01,792
[Jeff chuckles]

419
00:29:01,875 --> 00:29:04,292
I'm just asking if you take
any medication like,

420
00:29:04,958 --> 00:29:06,667
like prescriptions.

421
00:29:08,750 --> 00:29:10,292
No.

422
00:29:11,167 --> 00:29:12,583
You think I should?

423
00:29:12,667 --> 00:29:15,083
No. I didn't say that.

424
00:29:15,167 --> 00:29:17,750
I just, I just want you to feel good.

425
00:29:18,667 --> 00:29:20,000
Yeah, um...

426
00:29:20,667 --> 00:29:24,208
Okay. I-I-I do.

427
00:29:25,750 --> 00:29:27,542
I feel good.

428
00:29:27,625 --> 00:29:29,458
"Just like I knew that I would."

429
00:29:38,958 --> 00:29:42,542
Well, can I get you anything else?

430
00:29:42,625 --> 00:29:47,958
Any more water or tea,
or... maybe some of Jeff's bourbon?

431
00:29:48,042 --> 00:29:51,417
Oh, thanks, Dad.

432
00:29:52,125 --> 00:29:54,000
I'm... I'm driving.

433
00:29:54,083 --> 00:30:00,208
But, uh... you know, it's getting late,
we should probably get going.

434
00:30:00,292 --> 00:30:02,250
Yeah. Yeah.

435
00:30:02,333 --> 00:30:05,417
[Father] Oh, gee, okay.

436
00:30:05,500 --> 00:30:09,208
Well, I-I-I could make dinner.

437
00:30:09,292 --> 00:30:10,917
I have the pasta.

438
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:16,458
I have the... spaghetti sauce
which has the cheese already in it.

439
00:30:17,708 --> 00:30:20,542
I could-- Oh!
Or even you know...

440
00:30:20,625 --> 00:30:22,417
You remember cookie chicken?

441
00:30:22,500 --> 00:30:24,708
I remember cookie chicken.

442
00:30:24,792 --> 00:30:27,958
- Oh, yeah.
- You think I can make cookie chicken?

443
00:30:28,042 --> 00:30:30,000
Or ramen, or...

444
00:30:30,750 --> 00:30:32,625
I act-- I wish we could.

445
00:30:32,708 --> 00:30:34,708
- [Father, sighing] Oh.
- Hm.

446
00:30:36,583 --> 00:30:38,667
But... maybe next time.

447
00:30:39,625 --> 00:30:41,458
Yeah. Good.

448
00:30:41,542 --> 00:30:43,417
- [Jeff] Yeah.
- Okay.

449
00:30:45,292 --> 00:30:47,833
I'm sorry.
I wish you could stay longer.

450
00:30:47,917 --> 00:30:50,500
It's been really great seeing you.

451
00:30:54,958 --> 00:30:56,750
You really gotta go?

452
00:30:56,833 --> 00:30:58,375
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

453
00:30:58,875 --> 00:31:00,708
Well, gee, I...

454
00:31:02,375 --> 00:31:04,375
Well, okay.

455
00:31:16,250 --> 00:31:17,458
Okay.

456
00:31:18,583 --> 00:31:21,500
- Love you, darlin'.
- I love you, Daddy.

457
00:31:21,583 --> 00:31:23,375
[Father] Thanks for comin' to see me.

458
00:31:27,125 --> 00:31:28,542
Okay. Love you.

459
00:31:28,625 --> 00:31:30,833
Okay. I love you, too, son.

460
00:31:31,500 --> 00:31:32,750
Thanks for coming.

461
00:31:33,667 --> 00:31:35,667
- Great seeing you.
- Good seeing you.

462
00:31:37,875 --> 00:31:39,875
- To tide you over.
- Oh! No.

463
00:31:40,792 --> 00:31:42,208
No, no.

464
00:31:44,083 --> 00:31:45,833
I really appreciate it, son.

465
00:31:45,917 --> 00:31:47,542
Yeah.

466
00:31:47,625 --> 00:31:49,625
- Okay.
- Okay.

467
00:31:58,250 --> 00:31:59,500
[Jeff] Dad, your truck.

468
00:32:01,208 --> 00:32:02,208
What?

469
00:32:02,292 --> 00:32:04,583
Kinda looks like shit.
Does it even run?

470
00:32:04,667 --> 00:32:06,167
[Father] Oh, yeah. The Chevy?

471
00:32:06,250 --> 00:32:08,167
Oh, yeah. It's a little rough.

472
00:32:08,250 --> 00:32:11,042
A little rough,
but she's an old workhorse.

473
00:32:12,875 --> 00:32:13,958
[Jeff] Really?

474
00:32:14,750 --> 00:32:16,375
Doesn't look like it.

475
00:32:17,792 --> 00:32:20,083
Hey, nice ride, son.

476
00:32:20,667 --> 00:32:23,667
Oh, yeah. It's a hybrid.

477
00:32:24,833 --> 00:32:26,125
I lease it.

478
00:32:27,000 --> 00:32:28,458
Hybrid.

479
00:32:29,125 --> 00:32:30,875
[Jeff] Well, great to see you, Dad.

480
00:32:30,958 --> 00:32:33,667
Great to see you, son.
Love you, darlin'.

481
00:32:33,750 --> 00:32:35,750
- Bye, Daddy.
- [Father] Bye.

482
00:32:37,458 --> 00:32:39,042
Love you.

483
00:32:39,125 --> 00:32:42,333
Hey, thanks for coming out
and seeing your old Dad!

484
00:32:43,083 --> 00:32:44,250
[Jeff] Yeah.

485
00:32:46,792 --> 00:32:49,000
[Engine starts]

486
00:32:52,250 --> 00:32:54,458
[Car horn beeps]

487
00:33:24,208 --> 00:33:25,583
[He sighs]

488
00:33:25,667 --> 00:33:28,667
Oh, this really is some quality stuff.

489
00:33:29,458 --> 00:33:31,875
[He mumbles to himself]

490
00:33:39,958 --> 00:33:41,583
Yep. Yep.

491
00:33:42,583 --> 00:33:44,542
[He groans]

492
00:33:59,125 --> 00:34:00,750
Well, that was strange.

493
00:34:01,375 --> 00:34:03,125
I don't really know what to think.

494
00:34:03,208 --> 00:34:04,792
[Jeff] I think he was happy to see us.

495
00:34:07,333 --> 00:34:09,375
But as they say,
you can choose your friends,

496
00:34:09,458 --> 00:34:12,292
and your lovers
but you can't choose your family.

497
00:34:15,583 --> 00:34:17,708
I wasn't talking about you, Em.

498
00:34:18,333 --> 00:34:20,250
It's okay.
I know what you meant.

499
00:34:24,250 --> 00:34:26,292
He's always been a real character.

500
00:34:26,958 --> 00:34:28,375
A little mysterious.

501
00:34:28,458 --> 00:34:29,667
Yeah.

502
00:34:33,125 --> 00:34:36,125
Did he act kinda crazy
even when we were little, though?

503
00:34:37,292 --> 00:34:40,292
Yeah, but usually in sort of a fun way.

504
00:34:40,375 --> 00:34:41,708
[He huffs]

505
00:34:41,792 --> 00:34:45,333
But later on, he wasn't really
in my life all that much.

506
00:34:45,417 --> 00:34:49,583
I... I kind of lost the connection.

507
00:34:50,583 --> 00:34:52,917
[Jeff] I always wanted to look up to him.

508
00:34:53,458 --> 00:34:55,125
I tried to, anyway.

509
00:35:00,333 --> 00:35:03,375
Well, I'm glad we saw him.
Thanks for doing that.

510
00:35:03,458 --> 00:35:05,708
Sure. I'm glad too.

511
00:35:07,417 --> 00:35:08,542
Sort of.

512
00:35:16,417 --> 00:35:18,792
Did you see that Rolex, though?

513
00:35:18,875 --> 00:35:20,167
That was real.

514
00:35:20,250 --> 00:35:22,750
- Nah.
- Yep.

515
00:35:25,583 --> 00:35:26,708
Really?

516
00:35:43,333 --> 00:35:46,292
[Footsteps approach]

517
00:35:59,958 --> 00:36:02,292
[He mutters to himself]

518
00:36:09,333 --> 00:36:12,417
Hey, Charlotte. Yeah. What's up?

519
00:36:13,917 --> 00:36:16,667
Oh, yeah? That's interesting.

520
00:36:17,625 --> 00:36:19,500
Hey, listen.

521
00:36:20,250 --> 00:36:21,875
You...

522
00:36:21,958 --> 00:36:24,417
you know,
I came into some unexpected cash,

523
00:36:24,500 --> 00:36:28,542
and I wondered if you wanted to meet me
down at Shades for dinner and drinks?

524
00:36:28,625 --> 00:36:29,958
The whole bit.

525
00:36:31,208 --> 00:36:33,625
Yeah. My treat, of course.

526
00:36:33,708 --> 00:36:35,500
Yeah, um...

527
00:36:36,083 --> 00:36:38,500
Oh, good.
It'd be good to see you, too.

528
00:36:39,000 --> 00:36:40,167
Um.

529
00:36:40,875 --> 00:36:44,708
Listen, you get there before I do,
tell Tommy we want the usual booth.

530
00:36:44,792 --> 00:36:46,500
You know the routine.

531
00:36:47,583 --> 00:36:50,708
Yeah. Good.
Alright. Twenty minutes.

532
00:36:52,042 --> 00:36:54,125
I'll see you soon, baby.

533
00:36:54,208 --> 00:36:55,583
Bye.

534
00:36:57,875 --> 00:36:59,792
[He inhales]

535
00:36:59,875 --> 00:37:01,750
[He exhales]

536
00:37:01,833 --> 00:37:04,500
[Soft, pensive music]

537
00:37:12,625 --> 00:37:13,958
Okay.

538
00:37:15,000 --> 00:37:17,417
[He inhales, exhales loudly]

539
00:37:28,083 --> 00:37:29,500
A little on the...

540
00:37:37,417 --> 00:37:39,625
[Engine starts]

541
00:38:03,208 --> 00:38:05,708
[Music fades]

542
00:38:14,042 --> 00:38:16,000
[Delicate chimes]

543
00:38:17,250 --> 00:38:19,750
[Soft ambient music joins]

544
00:39:11,417 --> 00:39:13,917
[Music concludes, fades]

545
00:39:14,000 --> 00:39:16,458
[Birds cheep;
Dog barks in distance]

546
00:39:19,125 --> 00:39:20,792
[Indistinct voice on phone]

547
00:39:20,875 --> 00:39:24,500
Yeah, well, I'm very happy
to see them on the one hand, but...

548
00:39:26,083 --> 00:39:29,083
I just have to keep them
from stirring things up, you know?

549
00:39:30,625 --> 00:39:31,750
Yeah.

550
00:39:31,833 --> 00:39:33,250
Yeah, I still see them.

551
00:39:34,125 --> 00:39:37,958
Now once a year,
together, for afternoon tea.

552
00:39:38,042 --> 00:39:39,667
Our yearly ritual.

553
00:39:41,583 --> 00:39:44,792
Yeah, it really was
an excellent suggestion on your part.

554
00:39:45,750 --> 00:39:46,792
Mm-hmm.

555
00:39:46,875 --> 00:39:50,292
And we do speak on the phone.
Every few weeks at least.

556
00:39:54,958 --> 00:39:58,792
Well, Timothea calls
more frequently than Lilith.

557
00:40:00,250 --> 00:40:03,500
Yeah. She... she seems
more in need of me,

558
00:40:03,583 --> 00:40:05,667
even though she's the older one,
you know.

559
00:40:06,208 --> 00:40:09,042
At times,
she seems totally lost in the world.

560
00:40:09,917 --> 00:40:12,417
But somehow, I...

561
00:40:12,500 --> 00:40:15,125
I worry more about Lilith.

562
00:40:15,208 --> 00:40:17,417
Hm.

563
00:40:17,500 --> 00:40:20,208
[Clock chimes]

564
00:40:22,083 --> 00:40:25,500
Oh, dear. Our time is almost up,
and they'll be arriving shortly.

565
00:40:26,417 --> 00:40:28,625
Thank you so much
for doing a phone session.

566
00:40:29,917 --> 00:40:34,125
You are so very helpful, like a navigator.

567
00:40:36,167 --> 00:40:37,583
Yeah.

568
00:40:37,667 --> 00:40:39,833
Yeah, I'll see you next week, then?

569
00:40:41,250 --> 00:40:42,917
Thank you. Next Tuesday.

570
00:40:44,167 --> 00:40:45,500
Bye now. Bye.

571
00:40:50,875 --> 00:40:53,667
[Grandfather clock ticks softly]

572
00:41:05,333 --> 00:41:06,958
[Brakes squeak]

573
00:41:21,833 --> 00:41:23,958
[Seagulls shriek]

574
00:41:39,292 --> 00:41:41,792
[Loud clunk;
Engine hum cuts out]

575
00:41:56,333 --> 00:41:58,167
[Engine stops]

576
00:42:01,208 --> 00:42:03,125
Oh... darn it.

577
00:42:11,375 --> 00:42:13,542
That's the ID number.

578
00:42:13,625 --> 00:42:15,667
Yeah. Correct.

579
00:42:15,750 --> 00:42:18,042
Uh... Murtagh Road.

580
00:42:20,458 --> 00:42:23,500
Wha-- 45 to an hour?

581
00:42:23,583 --> 00:42:24,583
Oh, gosh.

582
00:42:25,167 --> 00:42:26,833
Okay, um.

583
00:42:26,917 --> 00:42:29,083
[Disconnected tone]

584
00:42:29,167 --> 00:42:30,250
Hello?

585
00:42:31,125 --> 00:42:33,458
[Baby gurgles in background]

586
00:42:40,417 --> 00:42:41,583
Hello, Mummy?

587
00:42:41,667 --> 00:42:43,750
[Mother, on phone] <i>Hello, dear.
Is everything alright?</i>

588
00:42:44,333 --> 00:42:47,542
[Woman] <i>No, actually.
My wretched car has broken down.</i>

589
00:42:47,625 --> 00:42:49,875
<i>I'm stuck by the side of the road.</i>

590
00:42:49,958 --> 00:42:51,833
Oh, no. I'm so sorry.

591
00:42:51,917 --> 00:42:53,958
<i>I'm going to be late now.</i>

592
00:42:54,042 --> 00:42:55,667
- Don't worry.
- [Woman] <i>I'm so sorry.</i>

593
00:42:55,750 --> 00:42:57,208
Lilith's not here either,

594
00:42:57,292 --> 00:42:59,708
and she's often late anyway.

595
00:42:59,792 --> 00:43:01,125
[Woman] <i>Ha, yes.</i>

596
00:43:01,208 --> 00:43:03,250
You don't need me
to come get you, do you?

597
00:43:03,333 --> 00:43:06,000
<i>No. I've called
the Automobile Association already.</i>

598
00:43:06,083 --> 00:43:09,042
Ah, Automobile Association to the rescue.

599
00:43:09,125 --> 00:43:10,292
[Woman] <i>Yes, indeed.</i>

600
00:43:10,375 --> 00:43:13,208
Okay. Take care, darling.
Thanks for calling.

601
00:43:13,292 --> 00:43:15,083
- See you soon.
- [Woman] <i>Thanks, Mummy.</i>

602
00:43:35,375 --> 00:43:38,292
Do you really only
see your ma once a year?

603
00:43:38,833 --> 00:43:40,833
And not even on a holiday?

604
00:43:41,708 --> 00:43:44,333
Yeah. No, we don't really
do holidays together.

605
00:43:46,125 --> 00:43:48,292
But you have tea.

606
00:43:48,375 --> 00:43:50,458
How very fucking traditional of you.

607
00:43:51,750 --> 00:43:55,458
It is kinda weird you and your sister
moved to Dublin to be near your ma,

608
00:43:55,542 --> 00:43:58,542
and then only see her, like, once a year.

609
00:43:58,625 --> 00:44:02,167
Yeah. I guess the set-up is kinda strange.

610
00:44:03,833 --> 00:44:05,833
Seems like you two grew up

611
00:44:05,917 --> 00:44:09,750
like butterflies floating
around each other in different directions.

612
00:44:12,083 --> 00:44:13,708
Do you ever miss living elsewhere?

613
00:44:13,792 --> 00:44:15,000
Yeah.

614
00:44:16,333 --> 00:44:18,750
Yeah, I miss Brussels.
I loved it there.

615
00:44:20,083 --> 00:44:21,583
Living all on my own.

616
00:44:22,167 --> 00:44:26,292
Yeah, you know you do sometimes
kinda sound, sort of Belgian.

617
00:44:27,500 --> 00:44:32,792
Yeah? Well, you always fucking sound

618
00:44:32,875 --> 00:44:33,875
- Irish!
- Irish.

619
00:44:33,958 --> 00:44:35,625
Yeah. I'm well aware of that.

620
00:44:42,625 --> 00:44:45,333
- I met your sister once.
- Yeah?

621
00:44:45,417 --> 00:44:46,875
Timothea, right?

622
00:44:46,958 --> 00:44:48,958
- Tim.
- She's nice.

623
00:44:49,708 --> 00:44:51,708
- Yeah.
- How is she?

624
00:44:53,875 --> 00:44:55,292
I don't know.

625
00:45:13,208 --> 00:45:14,417
[Loud rattling]

626
00:45:16,625 --> 00:45:19,375
[Dreamy ambient music]

627
00:45:43,167 --> 00:45:45,125
[Music fades]

628
00:45:45,750 --> 00:45:47,292
So when we get close,

629
00:45:47,375 --> 00:45:50,333
I just wanna jump
in back seat before we arrive, okay?

630
00:45:51,875 --> 00:45:53,208
And, why?

631
00:45:53,292 --> 00:45:54,917
I thought I told you.

632
00:45:55,000 --> 00:45:56,958
'Cause if we pull up
and my mum sees us,

633
00:45:57,042 --> 00:45:58,792
I can tell her I took an Uber.

634
00:45:59,750 --> 00:46:01,958
So, I'm your Uber driver? Really?

635
00:46:05,208 --> 00:46:06,583
Well, you've got the hat.

636
00:46:06,667 --> 00:46:08,958
Fuck off!

637
00:46:11,917 --> 00:46:13,042
Sorry.

638
00:46:13,125 --> 00:46:16,000
I'll tell her my Lexus is in the dealers.

639
00:46:16,083 --> 00:46:18,708
- Your Lexus?
- Yeah! My new Lexus!

640
00:46:28,333 --> 00:46:31,000
[Woman 2] So, as your Uber driver,
am I supposed to pick you up?

641
00:46:31,625 --> 00:46:34,625
Doesn't your sister live
way far in the other direction?

642
00:46:34,708 --> 00:46:36,708
Yeah, she lives in Nowheresville.

643
00:46:37,750 --> 00:46:39,750
No, I'll ask my mum to call me an Uber

644
00:46:39,833 --> 00:46:42,167
and then it'll be on her account.

645
00:46:42,250 --> 00:46:44,000
So somewhere around here,

646
00:46:44,083 --> 00:46:46,250
you can pull over,
and I'll get in the back.

647
00:46:46,792 --> 00:46:48,167
Okay.

648
00:47:03,667 --> 00:47:05,250
My chauffeur.

649
00:47:05,333 --> 00:47:06,750
[Lilith] Fuck off.

650
00:47:20,583 --> 00:47:23,000
[Engine sputters]

651
00:47:27,792 --> 00:47:30,208
[Engine starts]

652
00:47:32,625 --> 00:47:38,458
Yes. I'd like to cancel a service call
I made a little while ago.

653
00:47:40,833 --> 00:47:42,500
Murtagh Road.

654
00:47:44,125 --> 00:47:48,708
Yes. 7-7-2-3-3-5-3.
Thank you.

655
00:47:49,667 --> 00:47:51,667
[She groans]

656
00:48:22,708 --> 00:48:24,000
[Doorbell rings]

657
00:48:24,083 --> 00:48:25,750
Got you.

658
00:48:26,250 --> 00:48:28,583
- Hi, Mum.
- Hello, Lilith.

659
00:48:29,792 --> 00:48:30,958
I like your hair.

660
00:48:31,042 --> 00:48:32,667
Oh, thank you!

661
00:48:33,833 --> 00:48:35,667
I like your outfit, too.

662
00:48:37,000 --> 00:48:39,500
Yeah. Oh, you're ridiculous.

663
00:48:39,583 --> 00:48:40,708
[Lilith chuckles]

664
00:48:40,792 --> 00:48:42,208
Look at my bag.

665
00:48:43,417 --> 00:48:44,792
Designer bag.

666
00:48:44,875 --> 00:48:47,125
Yeah. It was on sale.

667
00:48:47,208 --> 00:48:49,042
Really?

668
00:48:49,125 --> 00:48:51,833
Place looks amazing. Love it.

669
00:48:51,917 --> 00:48:53,542
Well, thank you.

670
00:48:54,292 --> 00:48:56,542
Actually it's exactly
the same as last year.

671
00:48:56,625 --> 00:48:57,667
I'll take your-- oh!

672
00:48:57,750 --> 00:48:59,125
- Just here.
- Okay.

673
00:48:59,208 --> 00:49:00,292
Shall we sit and chat?

674
00:49:00,375 --> 00:49:02,375
- Until Tim arrives.
- Yeah. Here?

675
00:49:02,458 --> 00:49:04,292
- [Mother] Yeah.
- [Lilith] Yeah.

676
00:49:04,375 --> 00:49:06,542
She called to say she had car trouble.

677
00:49:06,625 --> 00:49:09,042
- Oh!
- And then she called to say she was fine.

678
00:49:09,125 --> 00:49:11,333
- So...
- Okay.

679
00:49:11,417 --> 00:49:12,833
So, I don't know.

680
00:49:13,750 --> 00:49:15,167
Yeah.

681
00:49:16,083 --> 00:49:18,625
And you? I saw you came
with a car service.

682
00:49:18,708 --> 00:49:22,208
Yeah. Yeah, well, I have a Lexus.

683
00:49:22,292 --> 00:49:24,750
A beautiful new one.
Almost new.

684
00:49:25,250 --> 00:49:26,917
But the dealer has it.

685
00:49:27,417 --> 00:49:29,792
It's being detailed.
It's a service they provide.

686
00:49:29,875 --> 00:49:31,667
- Is that right?
- Yeah.

687
00:49:32,875 --> 00:49:34,333
Well, that's a nice perk.

688
00:49:34,417 --> 00:49:37,083
Yeah, so I came in an Uber.

689
00:49:40,958 --> 00:49:42,792
So pretty.

690
00:49:44,375 --> 00:49:45,875
I was going to bring you some.

691
00:49:45,958 --> 00:49:49,042
But then the plan became awkward
with the Uber and everything.

692
00:49:49,708 --> 00:49:53,042
Well, it's sweet of you
to think of me in any case.

693
00:49:56,167 --> 00:49:58,500
So, how is everything?

694
00:49:59,542 --> 00:50:02,375
Are you still with
the gloriously handsome Robert?

695
00:50:02,458 --> 00:50:03,958
You never met him!

696
00:50:04,042 --> 00:50:06,375
[Mother] No, but you texted me photos.

697
00:50:06,458 --> 00:50:08,458
Without his shirt on, I might add.

698
00:50:08,542 --> 00:50:09,958
[Lilith] Yeah...

699
00:50:10,042 --> 00:50:11,417
it's a while ago.

700
00:50:12,042 --> 00:50:14,333
Well, he wants to marry me,

701
00:50:14,417 --> 00:50:16,042
but I don't know, you know.

702
00:50:16,125 --> 00:50:17,542
[Mother] Well, that's exciting.

703
00:50:17,625 --> 00:50:19,417
- You didn't tell me.
- No.

704
00:50:20,083 --> 00:50:23,167
Well, what are your thoughts?

705
00:50:23,250 --> 00:50:25,667
[Lilith] I have mixed thoughts, really.

706
00:50:25,750 --> 00:50:28,333
He is rich, and handsome.

707
00:50:29,000 --> 00:50:32,000
And says he adores me too.
And seems to.

708
00:50:32,083 --> 00:50:33,542
[Mother] Oh, I should hope so.

709
00:50:33,625 --> 00:50:35,000
Yeah.

710
00:50:35,083 --> 00:50:36,625
[Mother] Well, how do you feel?

711
00:50:39,083 --> 00:50:40,792
I don't know, Mum.

712
00:50:40,875 --> 00:50:45,208
I'm not sure if I wanna be,
you know, tied to a man.

713
00:50:45,917 --> 00:50:47,583
[Mother] Hmm, well...

714
00:50:47,667 --> 00:50:50,292
You have to be sure, I guess.

715
00:50:51,000 --> 00:50:52,875
If you want to make it work.

716
00:50:53,667 --> 00:50:54,917
[Lilith] Yeah.

717
00:50:57,250 --> 00:50:58,833
Yeah.

718
00:50:58,917 --> 00:51:01,083
[Grandfather clock ticks softly]

719
00:51:09,375 --> 00:51:11,583
[Vehicle approaches]

720
00:51:15,542 --> 00:51:16,875
Your sister is here.

721
00:51:18,042 --> 00:51:19,208
[Lilith] Oh, wonderful.

722
00:51:36,542 --> 00:51:37,542
Hello, Mummy.

723
00:51:37,625 --> 00:51:40,292
Timothea. How lovely to see you.

724
00:51:41,625 --> 00:51:43,208
[Mother chuckles softly]

725
00:51:46,875 --> 00:51:49,750
[Timothea chuckles;
Door closes]

726
00:51:51,958 --> 00:51:53,917
Look who's here!

727
00:51:54,458 --> 00:51:55,958
Oh.

728
00:51:56,042 --> 00:51:57,417
Hello, Tim.

729
00:51:57,500 --> 00:51:59,000
Hello, Lilith.

730
00:51:59,083 --> 00:52:00,750
[Timothea chuckles softly]

731
00:52:01,958 --> 00:52:03,625
You look fantastic!

732
00:52:03,708 --> 00:52:06,542
Oh, my, I don't know
about that, but you do.

733
00:52:07,458 --> 00:52:10,000
- Oh, it's so nice to see you.
- Yeah.

734
00:52:10,083 --> 00:52:11,542
Let me take these beautiful flowers.

735
00:52:11,625 --> 00:52:12,917
- Yes.
- Thank you.

736
00:52:13,458 --> 00:52:15,042
I'll take your coat.

737
00:52:16,292 --> 00:52:17,542
Okay.

738
00:52:17,625 --> 00:52:19,250
[Timothea clears her throat]

739
00:52:19,958 --> 00:52:21,625
[Lilith chuckles softly]

740
00:52:22,208 --> 00:52:25,708
Well, we seem to be
accidentally color coordinated.

741
00:52:26,375 --> 00:52:27,875
How embarrassing.

742
00:52:28,417 --> 00:52:31,458
Oh. Yes, it is, isn't it?

743
00:52:31,542 --> 00:52:33,500
- I love it.
- Do you really?

744
00:52:33,583 --> 00:52:35,000
Yeah. Red.

745
00:52:36,375 --> 00:52:38,875
- I'll put these lovely flowers in a vase.
- Yes.

746
00:52:42,625 --> 00:52:44,500
So, how is everything?

747
00:52:45,292 --> 00:52:48,500
Lil, it's... all good?

748
00:52:49,417 --> 00:52:50,458
- Yeah.
- I hope?

749
00:52:50,542 --> 00:52:51,667
Yeah. Fantastic.

750
00:52:51,750 --> 00:52:53,167
Yeah. Good.

751
00:52:53,250 --> 00:52:57,750
I almost hate to say it,
but my life's kinda been like a dream.

752
00:52:57,833 --> 00:52:58,917
Oh, great.

753
00:52:59,000 --> 00:53:01,792
- Yeah.
- Good. That, that's great to hear.

754
00:53:01,875 --> 00:53:03,000
And you?

755
00:53:03,083 --> 00:53:04,792
Me? Oh, well, oh...

756
00:53:05,542 --> 00:53:07,208
You know. Ups and downs.

757
00:53:07,292 --> 00:53:10,875
But everything's, everything's, it's good.

758
00:53:11,833 --> 00:53:13,083
- I think.
- Yeah?

759
00:53:13,167 --> 00:53:15,458
Yeah. I love your hair.

760
00:53:15,542 --> 00:53:17,042
Thank you.

761
00:53:17,125 --> 00:53:19,167
- So cute.
- [Lilith] Yours too.

762
00:53:19,250 --> 00:53:22,208
[Timothea] Yes. So, um...

763
00:53:24,417 --> 00:53:26,833
- Mum looks good.
- Yeah.

764
00:53:26,917 --> 00:53:30,125
- Doesn't she?
- Yeah. She looks great.

765
00:53:30,708 --> 00:53:32,917
She doesn't look old to you, does she?

766
00:53:33,000 --> 00:53:34,458
Ah, no.

767
00:53:36,792 --> 00:53:38,708
I... I think she looks great.

768
00:53:38,792 --> 00:53:40,292
- Yeah, she does.
- Yes.

769
00:53:40,375 --> 00:53:41,917
Yeah. Amazing.

770
00:53:51,833 --> 00:53:53,125
<i>Reckless Moonlight.</i>

771
00:53:53,208 --> 00:53:54,875
Do you know about this one?

772
00:53:55,375 --> 00:53:58,042
Yes. I saw it on the bestseller list.

773
00:53:58,958 --> 00:54:00,500
Put it back.

774
00:54:00,583 --> 00:54:04,792
Lilith. You know she doesn't like talking
about her books with us.

775
00:54:04,875 --> 00:54:06,500
I know, I know.

776
00:54:07,125 --> 00:54:09,125
<i>The Boundaries of Love.</i>

777
00:54:14,917 --> 00:54:18,542
- And <i>Lilacs In The Snow</i>.
- [She whispers] Lilith, stop it, please!

778
00:54:22,208 --> 00:54:23,208
[Timothea gasps softly]

779
00:54:23,292 --> 00:54:25,583
<i>An Unfaithful Tomorrow.</i>

780
00:54:27,125 --> 00:54:28,333
Girls.

781
00:54:30,000 --> 00:54:32,000
Shall we go to the table?

782
00:54:40,583 --> 00:54:41,667
Hey.

783
00:54:43,875 --> 00:54:46,083
Wait for Mummy.

784
00:55:12,375 --> 00:55:14,417
No. No, this won't do.

785
00:55:21,792 --> 00:55:22,833
Oh!

786
00:55:24,000 --> 00:55:25,458
That's much better.

787
00:55:25,542 --> 00:55:27,000
But your flowers were much more--

788
00:55:27,083 --> 00:55:28,083
Loud.

789
00:55:28,167 --> 00:55:30,125
[Mother] Well, let's say prominent.

790
00:55:30,208 --> 00:55:32,333
No, no. These are lovely.

791
00:55:32,917 --> 00:55:34,792
It's perfect. Much better.

792
00:55:35,708 --> 00:55:37,500
[Mother clears her throat]

793
00:55:41,875 --> 00:55:43,667
[Timothea clears her throat]

794
00:55:48,542 --> 00:55:50,333
[Lilith clears her throat]

795
00:55:56,000 --> 00:55:58,417
So, shall I be mother?

796
00:55:59,542 --> 00:56:01,208
You might as well start sometime.

797
00:56:01,292 --> 00:56:04,208
[Mother chuckles]

798
00:56:07,958 --> 00:56:09,625
[Timothea] Oh. Thank you.

799
00:56:15,208 --> 00:56:16,833
Mummy?

800
00:56:16,917 --> 00:56:18,333
Thank you.

801
00:56:24,583 --> 00:56:26,208
It's wonderful to see you both.

802
00:56:27,000 --> 00:56:28,625
Thank you for coming.

803
00:56:29,958 --> 00:56:31,167
Thank you, Mummy.

804
00:56:31,250 --> 00:56:32,583
Thanks, Mum.

805
00:56:38,250 --> 00:56:39,333
Mm.

806
00:56:43,458 --> 00:56:44,458
[Timothea] Lil!

807
00:56:45,250 --> 00:56:47,000
Fuck, that is so good!

808
00:56:48,333 --> 00:56:50,208
That looks delicious.

809
00:56:50,292 --> 00:56:52,292
Where, where do you get that from again?

810
00:56:52,375 --> 00:56:54,208
Oh, there's my very favourite
little shop

811
00:56:54,292 --> 00:56:57,125
in town that always
has all the things I like.

812
00:56:57,208 --> 00:56:59,875
You know, Walkers' shortbread fingers,

813
00:57:00,458 --> 00:57:03,042
MacKay's marmalade, PG Tips tea.

814
00:57:03,917 --> 00:57:07,083
And sometimes they just have
these delicious little cakes.

815
00:57:07,667 --> 00:57:11,292
So, whenever I crave one
of my favourite treats,

816
00:57:11,375 --> 00:57:13,000
I just pop down there,

817
00:57:13,833 --> 00:57:15,500
and your uncle's name is Robert!

818
00:57:15,583 --> 00:57:17,583
[All laugh lightly]

819
00:57:19,750 --> 00:57:22,583
Mmm, love 'em!

820
00:57:24,917 --> 00:57:26,500
Oh, it's heavenly.

821
00:57:29,333 --> 00:57:30,750
[She slurps loudly]

822
00:57:39,167 --> 00:57:40,625
[Lilith] Mm.

823
00:57:45,667 --> 00:57:47,333
[Timothea] Mm.

824
00:57:53,708 --> 00:57:54,917
Lil!

825
00:57:55,000 --> 00:57:56,042
Sorry, I really...

826
00:57:56,625 --> 00:57:58,292
I just, I have this big sale online

827
00:57:58,375 --> 00:58:00,583
of some vintage clothes,
and I have to close it.

828
00:58:01,583 --> 00:58:03,667
Lil, not at table.

829
00:58:03,750 --> 00:58:07,000
Mmm. This tea is so good, Mummy.

830
00:58:10,833 --> 00:58:12,917
There. All done.

831
00:58:14,125 --> 00:58:15,750
It is.

832
00:58:15,833 --> 00:58:17,833
I think the water makes
all the difference.

833
00:58:17,917 --> 00:58:20,833
Do you use bottled or filtered water?

834
00:58:20,917 --> 00:58:23,917
I try to drink only pure bottled water
from Iceland.

835
00:58:24,000 --> 00:58:26,542
From Iceland?
Is that right?

836
00:58:26,625 --> 00:58:28,708
Well, for tea this water
has been boiled,

837
00:58:28,792 --> 00:58:30,333
but the water here is filtered.

838
00:58:30,417 --> 00:58:32,167
That's good to know.

839
00:58:32,875 --> 00:58:36,000
- I still drink tap water, so...
- [Lilith gasps]

840
00:58:36,083 --> 00:58:38,125
[Lilith] No, you shouldn't, Tim.

841
00:58:38,208 --> 00:58:40,917
I've heard people getting
their tap water tested,

842
00:58:41,000 --> 00:58:43,250
and they found horrible things in it.

843
00:58:43,333 --> 00:58:46,500
You know, like cocaine, Covid,

844
00:58:46,583 --> 00:58:49,833
E. Coli, amoebas, antibiotics,

845
00:58:49,917 --> 00:58:51,875
fentanyl, all kinds of shit.

846
00:58:51,958 --> 00:58:53,708
Lilith, that's terrible.

847
00:58:54,542 --> 00:58:56,833
Where do you hear such things?

848
00:58:56,917 --> 00:59:00,250
Some... podcast or something.

849
00:59:00,333 --> 00:59:01,625
Oh, Lil.

850
00:59:06,583 --> 00:59:10,500
So, Tim,
what does your tap water taste like?

851
00:59:10,583 --> 00:59:13,625
Well, I don't know.
It just tastes like water.

852
00:59:13,708 --> 00:59:16,667
I mean, does water even have a taste?

853
00:59:16,750 --> 00:59:18,042
Well, of course it does.

854
00:59:18,125 --> 00:59:20,333
It tastes like... water.

855
00:59:20,417 --> 00:59:21,625
[Timothea] Yeah.

856
00:59:22,917 --> 00:59:24,750
[Softly] Hm.

857
00:59:33,792 --> 00:59:35,708
So, girls...

858
00:59:36,583 --> 00:59:38,292
please give me some news.

859
00:59:39,833 --> 00:59:41,833
How's everything going?

860
00:59:56,000 --> 00:59:57,792
Oh, um...

861
00:59:59,542 --> 01:00:00,958
You know...

862
01:00:01,792 --> 01:00:06,750
Each year I check my Chinese horoscope,

863
01:00:06,833 --> 01:00:09,292
- and I'm a Sheep, you know?
- And a Capricorn.

864
01:00:09,375 --> 01:00:13,333
Yes. And every year
it says the same thing. It says,

865
01:00:13,417 --> 01:00:17,208
"Another year with numerous
minor obstacles

866
01:00:17,292 --> 01:00:21,750
"but the sheep will remain good-natured,
and you know, plod along."

867
01:00:21,833 --> 01:00:23,667
Does it really say "plod along"?

868
01:00:23,750 --> 01:00:25,500
Yes, well, something like that.

869
01:00:25,583 --> 01:00:28,417
[Mother] But your good nature
is a wonderful gift, though.

870
01:00:29,667 --> 01:00:30,667
Thank you--

871
01:00:30,750 --> 01:00:32,458
My Chinese sign is...

872
01:00:32,542 --> 01:00:35,542
- A Dragon.
- A Dragon. Yes, we know that.

873
01:00:35,625 --> 01:00:39,000
And usually, it says the dragon
will have another fantastic year,

874
01:00:39,083 --> 01:00:40,917
with adventure and romance.

875
01:00:41,500 --> 01:00:42,792
And is this true?

876
01:00:42,875 --> 01:00:45,625
Hm, yeah.
No complaints really.

877
01:00:45,708 --> 01:00:47,208
[Mother] Hmm.

878
01:00:48,292 --> 01:00:50,208
[Birds chirp outside]

879
01:01:01,083 --> 01:01:02,542
[Timothea sighs]

880
01:01:05,250 --> 01:01:09,083
Well, I do have some good news
I'd like to share.

881
01:01:10,708 --> 01:01:12,083
Oh?

882
01:01:13,000 --> 01:01:14,292
[Timothea] I've been promoted.

883
01:01:15,333 --> 01:01:16,375
Tim!

884
01:01:16,458 --> 01:01:19,208
I'm now on the executive board
of the Heritage Council.

885
01:01:19,292 --> 01:01:20,750
Fantastic, Tim!

886
01:01:20,833 --> 01:01:22,833
Yes! You know the one that decides

887
01:01:22,917 --> 01:01:25,833
which old buildings
in Dublin will be protected.

888
01:01:25,917 --> 01:01:27,917
[Mother] Well, that's wonderful news!

889
01:01:28,833 --> 01:01:30,333
Have you started yet?

890
01:01:30,417 --> 01:01:34,083
Well... the first full meeting's
next week, but yes.

891
01:01:35,417 --> 01:01:37,708
I am very excited.

892
01:01:37,792 --> 01:01:39,958
- [Lilith] Cool.
- Yes.

893
01:01:40,042 --> 01:01:41,958
[Mother] I'm very proud of you, Tim.

894
01:01:42,042 --> 01:01:43,125
And all your work.

895
01:01:43,208 --> 01:01:44,958
And I always have been.

896
01:01:45,042 --> 01:01:47,458
Well, thank you, Mummy.
I really do appreciate that.

897
01:01:48,042 --> 01:01:49,125
Oh!

898
01:01:51,917 --> 01:01:53,792
I have some good news too!

899
01:01:56,250 --> 01:01:57,458
Do you?

900
01:01:58,417 --> 01:01:59,417
[Mother] Yes.

901
01:02:00,083 --> 01:02:02,167
So, I have a number of new clients.

902
01:02:02,250 --> 01:02:04,042
I call disciples.

903
01:02:04,125 --> 01:02:05,833
Part of my community.

904
01:02:05,917 --> 01:02:08,167
And they're all very popular influencers.

905
01:02:08,958 --> 01:02:11,375
Well... that sounds like a good idea.

906
01:02:11,458 --> 01:02:12,750
[Timothea] Influencers?

907
01:02:13,458 --> 01:02:15,375
Can you explain that to me?

908
01:02:16,125 --> 01:02:17,750
[Lilith] Oh boy, not now.

909
01:02:18,417 --> 01:02:21,167
I'm sorry.
I didn't want to interrupt your news, Tim.

910
01:02:21,250 --> 01:02:24,250
I can tell you both about it another time.

911
01:02:26,542 --> 01:02:28,417
But we are interested.

912
01:02:32,250 --> 01:02:34,000
[She sighs, softly]

913
01:02:46,292 --> 01:02:48,292
[Teacups clink]

914
01:03:05,625 --> 01:03:06,750
May I be excused?

915
01:03:06,833 --> 01:03:09,000
- I just...
- Yes, certainly, dear.

916
01:03:09,083 --> 01:03:10,500
I need to go to the loo.

917
01:03:22,875 --> 01:03:24,458
[Mother] Tell me a joke.

918
01:03:25,625 --> 01:03:29,000
[Lilith] Mm, so, in the universe,

919
01:03:29,083 --> 01:03:32,167
three planets are floating,
and they're having a grand time,

920
01:03:32,250 --> 01:03:34,833
having tea, PG tips.

921
01:03:34,917 --> 01:03:36,667
"Oh, how're you?",
"I'm good. How're you?"

922
01:03:36,750 --> 01:03:40,250
And then there's a third planet
that comes along, and looks like shit.

923
01:03:40,792 --> 01:03:42,333
Coughing.

924
01:03:42,417 --> 01:03:45,542
And they say, "What is it with you?
Why are you looking so shit?"

925
01:03:45,625 --> 01:03:48,625
And then he says, "Oh, you know,

926
01:03:48,708 --> 01:03:50,583
"it's just that I've caught this thing.

927
01:03:50,667 --> 01:03:52,792
"It's called humanity."

928
01:03:52,875 --> 01:03:56,833
And then they laugh,
and they say, "Oh, that's okay.

929
01:03:56,917 --> 01:03:58,333
"That one goes away."

930
01:03:58,417 --> 01:04:00,583
[Mother laughs]

931
01:04:01,083 --> 01:04:03,083
- That is good!
- [Lilith] Yeah.

932
01:04:04,333 --> 01:04:06,083
[She breathes shakily]

933
01:04:20,750 --> 01:04:22,333
Hm.

934
01:04:25,417 --> 01:04:27,917
[Mother and Lilith laugh downstairs]

935
01:04:29,875 --> 01:04:32,375
[Mother and Lilith talk indistinctly]

936
01:04:35,875 --> 01:04:37,375
Like my new watch?

937
01:04:38,208 --> 01:04:40,333
Oh, yes. That's nice.

938
01:04:41,417 --> 01:04:42,583
Oh, my!

939
01:04:44,583 --> 01:04:45,875
Is that a Rolex?

940
01:04:45,958 --> 01:04:47,083
Yes.

941
01:04:47,167 --> 01:04:49,167
Oh! Very nice.

942
01:04:53,042 --> 01:04:55,333
You can't tell it's fake, can you?

943
01:04:55,917 --> 01:04:59,292
I... I bought it on Moore Street
from some guys.

944
01:04:59,375 --> 01:05:00,417
Don't you love it?

945
01:05:00,500 --> 01:05:02,042
Hmm.

946
01:05:22,250 --> 01:05:24,708
Oh, it's later than I thought.

947
01:05:25,292 --> 01:05:26,875
Oh, it's not. Is it really?

948
01:05:26,958 --> 01:05:29,167
But it seems you only just got here.

949
01:05:30,250 --> 01:05:32,458
Yes. We've only just got here.

950
01:05:33,833 --> 01:05:35,958
Well, time flies when you're having fun.

951
01:05:47,000 --> 01:05:48,333
[Lilith clears her throat]

952
01:05:53,708 --> 01:05:56,708
[Soft, uneasy music]

953
01:06:05,667 --> 01:06:08,042
[Music increases]

954
01:06:27,875 --> 01:06:30,417
[Music fades]

955
01:06:42,583 --> 01:06:44,583
Some little things for each of you.

956
01:06:45,167 --> 01:06:47,042
With some of the cakes you like.

957
01:06:47,125 --> 01:06:48,542
[Timothea] Oh.

958
01:06:50,833 --> 01:06:53,458
I hope your little car
will be running perfectly.

959
01:06:53,542 --> 01:06:55,792
Oh, yes. I think it's fine. Fine.

960
01:06:55,875 --> 01:06:57,708
And I assume you called an Uber.

961
01:06:57,792 --> 01:06:59,542
Um...

962
01:07:00,208 --> 01:07:03,208
Hey, Lil, would you like me
to give you a lift home?

963
01:07:03,292 --> 01:07:06,458
Oh, no, no. Thanks, sis.

964
01:07:06,542 --> 01:07:08,167
Not sure your car would make it.

965
01:07:08,875 --> 01:07:12,208
No, that's sweet of you,
but it's an hour in the other direction.

966
01:07:12,292 --> 01:07:16,292
And anyway, I was going to make
some important calls on the way back.

967
01:07:17,250 --> 01:07:19,417
So, um... Mum,

968
01:07:19,958 --> 01:07:22,958
I feel really bad to even ask,
but I had some trouble

969
01:07:23,042 --> 01:07:26,125
with my Uber account on the way here,
so, I was wondering--

970
01:07:26,208 --> 01:07:29,083
But, dear, they brought you here,
so I'm sure it's fine.

971
01:07:29,167 --> 01:07:31,542
Yeah, no. It's not going to work
on the way back.

972
01:07:31,625 --> 01:07:33,625
Yeah. I can't explain really.

973
01:07:34,167 --> 01:07:37,125
But... if I could use your account.

974
01:07:38,333 --> 01:07:41,333
You know, I'm kinda stressed out about it.

975
01:07:42,333 --> 01:07:44,375
Of course.
Of course, darling.

976
01:07:44,458 --> 01:07:46,167
I'll call you one.

977
01:07:46,250 --> 01:07:48,750
- [She sighs]
- Thanks a million, Mum.

978
01:07:49,625 --> 01:07:51,250
I'll pay you back.

979
01:07:54,667 --> 01:07:56,792
There. You put in your destination.

980
01:07:56,875 --> 01:07:58,083
Okay.

981
01:08:05,500 --> 01:08:06,792
Seven minutes.

982
01:08:11,792 --> 01:08:14,208
I'll wait with you
til the Uber comes then.

983
01:08:16,833 --> 01:08:18,333
- Alright, then.
- Hm.

984
01:08:27,707 --> 01:08:30,875
[Clock chimes]

985
01:08:38,500 --> 01:08:39,792
Three minutes.

986
01:08:42,667 --> 01:08:44,667
[Seagulls shriek]

987
01:08:55,292 --> 01:08:57,707
[Engine approaches]

988
01:09:03,125 --> 01:09:04,667
Goodbye, darling.

989
01:09:04,750 --> 01:09:06,582
- Bye bye, Mummy.
- Bye.

990
01:09:15,207 --> 01:09:17,667
[Bird cheeps]

991
01:09:23,582 --> 01:09:26,582
[Soft, pensive music]

992
01:09:44,292 --> 01:09:46,582
[Engine starts]

993
01:10:25,792 --> 01:10:28,250
[Music fades]

994
01:10:37,542 --> 01:10:40,042
[Soft ambient music]

995
01:11:40,458 --> 01:11:43,083
[Music fades]

996
01:11:57,792 --> 01:12:00,792
[Dreamy ambient music]

997
01:12:18,417 --> 01:12:20,417
[Music fades]

998
01:12:21,583 --> 01:12:24,042
[Man, in French] Careful,
they're stronger than last time.

999
01:12:24,125 --> 01:12:26,458
- Okay. <i>A plus</i>.
- <i>A plus</i>.

1000
01:12:42,250 --> 01:12:45,250
[In English] Thanks for waiting.
Appreciate it.

1001
01:12:45,333 --> 01:12:46,500
[Sister] Seat belt.

1002
01:13:03,833 --> 01:13:05,958
So, did you meet your weed guy there?

1003
01:13:06,042 --> 01:13:08,500
[Brother] No, I get shrooms from him.

1004
01:13:08,583 --> 01:13:11,333
[Sister] You still smoke
a lot of weed these days?

1005
01:13:11,417 --> 01:13:14,250
Weed or hash rarely.
But shrooms, yes.

1006
01:13:14,333 --> 01:13:17,375
I micro-dose.
Little amounts each day.

1007
01:13:17,458 --> 01:13:19,542
I know what micro-dosing is.

1008
01:13:19,625 --> 01:13:22,042
[Train screeches]

1009
01:13:37,167 --> 01:13:39,167
I could sorta tell you were doing it.

1010
01:13:39,708 --> 01:13:41,083
- [Brother] Really?
- Yeah.

1011
01:13:41,167 --> 01:13:43,167
- [Brother] You could feel it?
- Mm-hmm.

1012
01:13:45,125 --> 01:13:46,417
Did you start recently?

1013
01:13:46,500 --> 01:13:48,583
Like maybe a month ago,
while I was in New York?

1014
01:13:48,667 --> 01:13:51,083
Exactly. Like four weeks ago.

1015
01:13:52,250 --> 01:13:55,583
So, you can even sense
the effects of a micro-dose?

1016
01:13:55,667 --> 01:13:58,500
Sorta. Twin factor.

1017
01:13:58,583 --> 01:13:59,875
Twin factor.

1018
01:13:59,958 --> 01:14:03,375
Plus that psilocybin is just
some crazy shit, bro.

1019
01:14:03,458 --> 01:14:04,750
[Brother] Saved my life in a way.

1020
01:14:04,833 --> 01:14:06,958
You know, it resets everything.

1021
01:14:07,042 --> 01:14:09,042
Brings everything back to zero.

1022
01:14:09,958 --> 01:14:13,500
Where each moment, is each moment.

1023
01:14:13,583 --> 01:14:17,542
- Yeah. I could use some of that.
- [Sirens wail nearby]

1024
01:14:17,625 --> 01:14:20,250
- [Brother] You smoke any weed these days?
- No, no.

1025
01:14:20,333 --> 01:14:23,000
I try to stay as clear
as possible these days.

1026
01:14:24,750 --> 01:14:26,417
Life is...

1027
01:14:27,042 --> 01:14:28,833
tripped out enough for me.

1028
01:14:28,917 --> 01:14:29,958
[Brother] Hm.

1029
01:14:39,583 --> 01:14:41,750
[Sister] So, this is gonna be kinda weird.

1030
01:14:42,625 --> 01:14:44,042
Going back to the apartment.

1031
01:14:46,167 --> 01:14:47,583
It'll be okay.

1032
01:14:51,458 --> 01:14:55,292
I... I guess it is kinda weird
with all their stuff cleared out.

1033
01:14:56,083 --> 01:14:58,542
Thanks again for doing it all.

1034
01:14:58,625 --> 01:14:59,917
It's all good.

1035
01:15:00,792 --> 01:15:01,833
It's Mom and Dad.

1036
01:15:19,333 --> 01:15:21,500
- Wanna get a coffee for a second?
- [Sister] Yeah.

1037
01:15:22,125 --> 01:15:23,292
Where should I go?

1038
01:15:23,375 --> 01:15:25,083
I don't know, uh...

1039
01:15:27,958 --> 01:15:30,167
Take a left up there.

1040
01:15:30,250 --> 01:15:32,750
[Car indicator ticks]

1041
01:15:47,583 --> 01:15:50,208
[Birds cheep;
People chat indistinctly]

1042
01:15:52,917 --> 01:15:54,250
[In French] Thank you.

1043
01:16:17,708 --> 01:16:19,417
[In English] Good idea to get a coffee.

1044
01:16:21,417 --> 01:16:23,208
It's comforting.

1045
01:16:23,292 --> 01:16:24,708
[Brother] Right?

1046
01:16:34,458 --> 01:16:36,833
Hey. Where are you?

1047
01:16:42,500 --> 01:16:44,708
I'm just in Nowheresville.

1048
01:16:44,792 --> 01:16:46,542
Nowhere special.

1049
01:16:46,625 --> 01:16:48,250
Yeah, I've been there.

1050
01:16:53,667 --> 01:16:55,250
What are you doing?

1051
01:16:55,333 --> 01:16:57,333
Just looking at this water.

1052
01:17:02,208 --> 01:17:03,500
And?

1053
01:17:04,708 --> 01:17:08,583
I was just... thinking about
this cool Indigenous girl I met.

1054
01:17:09,750 --> 01:17:13,417
She kept referring to water
as... medicine.

1055
01:17:13,542 --> 01:17:14,625
[Sister] Hmm.

1056
01:17:14,708 --> 01:17:17,375
I don't know. It just keeps
coming back to me.

1057
01:17:27,833 --> 01:17:29,375
Damn.

1058
01:17:32,375 --> 01:17:34,667
This whole world is so fragile.

1059
01:17:34,750 --> 01:17:36,583
Yeah, no shit.

1060
01:17:40,583 --> 01:17:42,583
Aren't you glad we have...

1061
01:17:44,667 --> 01:17:48,708
We had... such unconventional parents?

1062
01:17:48,792 --> 01:17:52,083
Yeah, I mean, who wants to be a square
and live a conventional life?

1063
01:17:52,167 --> 01:17:53,375
True.

1064
01:17:56,625 --> 01:17:59,792
I guess being twins is already
kind of unconventional.

1065
01:17:59,875 --> 01:18:01,083
Being your twin sure is.

1066
01:18:01,167 --> 01:18:04,250
I mean, I'm also sure
there's a bunch of twins out there

1067
01:18:04,333 --> 01:18:05,958
that are total fucking squares.

1068
01:18:06,625 --> 01:18:08,000
True.

1069
01:18:13,042 --> 01:18:14,500
This is a weird question,

1070
01:18:14,583 --> 01:18:15,583
[She inhales]

1071
01:18:15,667 --> 01:18:17,667
but do you think the plane
still would've crashed

1072
01:18:17,750 --> 01:18:19,750
if Mom was the one who was flying it?

1073
01:18:20,417 --> 01:18:21,708
Knowing her...

1074
01:18:23,125 --> 01:18:24,292
probably not.

1075
01:18:26,458 --> 01:18:31,583
They said it was some
weird weather-related thing.

1076
01:18:31,667 --> 01:18:33,708
Who knows.

1077
01:18:33,792 --> 01:18:36,208
Didn't we talk about this already?

1078
01:18:36,292 --> 01:18:38,417
Those small planes
are so fucking dangerous.

1079
01:18:38,500 --> 01:18:40,042
Yeah, well so are motorcycles.

1080
01:18:40,125 --> 01:18:42,792
Yeah, at least on a bike
I'm not way up in the fucking sky.

1081
01:18:42,875 --> 01:18:45,833
No. You're not. Not literally.

1082
01:18:47,542 --> 01:18:48,625
[He sighs]

1083
01:18:49,208 --> 01:18:51,000
I assume you still have your bike?

1084
01:18:51,083 --> 01:18:53,625
Yeah. My '84 Kawi 650.

1085
01:18:53,708 --> 01:18:55,375
[Sister] That beat-up old black thing?

1086
01:18:55,458 --> 01:18:57,542
- That's my baby.
- [Sister laughs softly]

1087
01:19:00,917 --> 01:19:03,000
[He sighs]

1088
01:19:06,125 --> 01:19:08,125
It's like you said, though.

1089
01:19:09,458 --> 01:19:11,458
The world is really fragile, I think.

1090
01:19:13,208 --> 01:19:15,000
Anything can happen. It's all so...

1091
01:19:15,750 --> 01:19:16,958
random.

1092
01:19:22,125 --> 01:19:25,458
So, how's your love life, Boobie?
You beating 'em back with a shovel?

1093
01:19:25,542 --> 01:19:27,125
Oh, shut up.

1094
01:19:27,208 --> 01:19:30,000
[Brother] I really do think you should
move back here for a while though.

1095
01:19:30,083 --> 01:19:32,417
Plus, you can keep their car.

1096
01:19:32,500 --> 01:19:33,833
I love that thing.

1097
01:19:33,917 --> 01:19:36,125
Classic Volvo 1800.

1098
01:19:36,208 --> 01:19:37,833
I don't want their car.

1099
01:19:38,500 --> 01:19:40,708
I'm not sure what I'm gonna do.

1100
01:19:41,292 --> 01:19:42,750
Well...

1101
01:19:42,833 --> 01:19:45,583
to quote the great Groucho Marx,

1102
01:19:45,667 --> 01:19:48,167
"Whatever it is, I'm against it."

1103
01:19:48,250 --> 01:19:50,042
[Sister laughs]

1104
01:19:50,125 --> 01:19:51,750
Yeah, no shit.

1105
01:19:51,833 --> 01:19:53,917
[Brother slurps loudly]

1106
01:20:00,583 --> 01:20:03,000
Can you toast with coffee, though?

1107
01:20:06,292 --> 01:20:08,083
It doesn't matter.

1108
01:20:08,167 --> 01:20:10,083
[Brother sniffs]

1109
01:20:27,625 --> 01:20:28,667
Ahh!

1110
01:20:29,208 --> 01:20:31,333
- [Both burp]
- Brutal.

1111
01:20:31,417 --> 01:20:33,000
Always.

1112
01:20:36,625 --> 01:20:38,333
Should we get the fuck outta here?

1113
01:20:38,417 --> 01:20:39,417
[Sister] Yeah, let's go.

1114
01:20:48,250 --> 01:20:49,792
[Brother, in French] Thank you.

1115
01:20:51,292 --> 01:20:52,542
Thank you.

1116
01:20:53,042 --> 01:20:54,250
[Waiter] Goodbye.

1117
01:21:01,333 --> 01:21:03,792
[Train horn blares]

1118
01:21:14,917 --> 01:21:16,417
[Brother, in English] Hey.

1119
01:21:17,083 --> 01:21:19,833
Do you feel any side effects
from that weird virus

1120
01:21:20,542 --> 01:21:22,167
now that it's been a few months?

1121
01:21:22,250 --> 01:21:24,042
No. Not really.

1122
01:21:25,875 --> 01:21:29,042
It was nice that you called
before I said anything though.

1123
01:21:29,125 --> 01:21:30,750
Yeah, it was weird.

1124
01:21:31,292 --> 01:21:33,875
Just for a moment,
I thought I was getting it too.

1125
01:21:33,958 --> 01:21:35,958
So... then I called you.

1126
01:21:36,958 --> 01:21:38,667
- Twin factor.
- Twin factor.

1127
01:21:41,458 --> 01:21:43,458
Everything good now though?

1128
01:21:43,542 --> 01:21:45,083
Virally speaking, yeah.

1129
01:21:49,458 --> 01:21:50,875
Oh shit.

1130
01:21:53,667 --> 01:21:55,167
What the fuck?

1131
01:21:55,875 --> 01:21:57,750
[Brother sighs]

1132
01:22:09,250 --> 01:22:11,458
[Sister] Aren't we, like, a block away?

1133
01:22:12,083 --> 01:22:13,583
It doesn't even look familiar.

1134
01:22:14,167 --> 01:22:16,542
Yeah. It all changes so fast.

1135
01:22:17,583 --> 01:22:18,833
It's all so fucking...

1136
01:22:18,917 --> 01:22:19,917
Impermanent.

1137
01:22:20,000 --> 01:22:21,208
Yeah.

1138
01:22:22,667 --> 01:22:24,417
We're almost there though.

1139
01:22:54,458 --> 01:22:56,458
[Car engine shuts off]

1140
01:23:01,083 --> 01:23:03,417
[Siren wails in distance]

1141
01:23:21,708 --> 01:23:23,250
Mom's old trick.

1142
01:23:23,333 --> 01:23:25,333
Yeah. Hope it still works.

1143
01:23:25,417 --> 01:23:27,042
[He exhales]

1144
01:23:32,625 --> 01:23:35,083
- [He clears his throat]
- [Electronic beeps]

1145
01:23:39,667 --> 01:23:41,000
Hey.

1146
01:23:45,458 --> 01:23:46,875
[In French] Hello.

1147
01:23:58,625 --> 01:24:01,417
[Footsteps, echoing]

1148
01:24:09,250 --> 01:24:12,333
I used to love this staircase
when we were kids.

1149
01:24:12,417 --> 01:24:13,708
[Brother] Same.

1150
01:24:15,000 --> 01:24:17,375
[Door lock clicks]

1151
01:24:43,833 --> 01:24:46,792
[Traffic hum increases]

1152
01:24:54,750 --> 01:24:56,667
You're dead.

1153
01:24:57,167 --> 01:24:58,958
You're dead.

1154
01:24:59,583 --> 01:25:02,083
[Soft, pensive music]

1155
01:25:33,917 --> 01:25:35,542
You alright?

1156
01:25:35,625 --> 01:25:36,833
Yeah.

1157
01:25:38,167 --> 01:25:40,458
Just thinking about Dad's cooking.

1158
01:25:40,542 --> 01:25:42,792
Yeah. It was good.

1159
01:25:42,875 --> 01:25:44,292
So good.

1160
01:25:50,667 --> 01:25:53,167
[Music continues;
Bell tolls]

1161
01:26:16,792 --> 01:26:18,417
Mom and Dad's room.

1162
01:26:26,375 --> 01:26:28,875
[Music fades]

1163
01:26:35,083 --> 01:26:37,583
[He inhales, exhales deeply]

1164
01:26:57,250 --> 01:26:59,750
[Soft, pensive music resumes]

1165
01:27:11,417 --> 01:27:14,417
[Music increases, undulates]

1166
01:27:35,917 --> 01:27:38,417
[Music concludes]

1167
01:27:41,542 --> 01:27:44,042
[Creaky footsteps]

1168
01:28:01,458 --> 01:28:02,958
Water?

1169
01:28:03,042 --> 01:28:05,042
- Medicine.
- Yep.

1170
01:28:12,417 --> 01:28:14,625
[She swallows loudly]

1171
01:28:23,583 --> 01:28:25,000
Really?

1172
01:28:25,083 --> 01:28:28,667
Listen, man,
I wouldn't wanna mix up our molecules.

1173
01:28:28,750 --> 01:28:30,500
Yeah, fuck you.

1174
01:28:30,583 --> 01:28:32,583
And your damn molecules.

1175
01:28:33,500 --> 01:28:35,500
Saved some shit I wanna show you.

1176
01:28:38,917 --> 01:28:42,250
Billy. You did all this
since we last spoke?

1177
01:28:42,833 --> 01:28:44,583
Yeah.

1178
01:28:44,667 --> 01:28:46,333
Organized it all.

1179
01:28:46,417 --> 01:28:48,000
Packed it all up.

1180
01:28:48,083 --> 01:28:50,292
Sent it to storage.
Packed.

1181
01:28:50,958 --> 01:28:52,875
And... about two truckloads.

1182
01:28:52,958 --> 01:28:55,917
Well, two vans anyways.

1183
01:28:56,000 --> 01:28:57,667
[Sister] Hmm.

1184
01:28:58,500 --> 01:29:00,292
That's amazing.

1185
01:29:00,375 --> 01:29:02,208
Thank you.

1186
01:29:02,917 --> 01:29:05,542
I am so impressed you organized it all.

1187
01:29:06,333 --> 01:29:08,875
Especially impressive for such a fuck up.

1188
01:29:08,958 --> 01:29:10,375
Thanks.

1189
01:29:11,125 --> 01:29:13,083
It wasn't actually that bad though.

1190
01:29:13,167 --> 01:29:17,417
Drove each load over,
packed into the storage unit, and...

1191
01:29:17,500 --> 01:29:19,500
- Bob's your uncle!
- Bob's your uncle!

1192
01:29:20,458 --> 01:29:22,083
I love you.

1193
01:29:26,000 --> 01:29:27,625
Check this out.

1194
01:29:30,750 --> 01:29:32,917
- [Sister gasps]
- This one first.

1195
01:29:35,125 --> 01:29:36,542
[Sister] Wow.

1196
01:29:39,583 --> 01:29:41,208
I love this.

1197
01:29:41,833 --> 01:29:43,208
I wanna keep this.

1198
01:29:43,917 --> 01:29:45,333
Take it.

1199
01:29:45,417 --> 01:29:46,833
I scanned all these.

1200
01:29:49,875 --> 01:29:52,833
[Sister] We didn't come out looking
that much alike, did we?

1201
01:29:52,917 --> 01:29:54,958
- [Brother] Really?
- [Sister] I don't think so.

1202
01:29:55,042 --> 01:29:56,333
It's a good thing, too.

1203
01:29:56,417 --> 01:29:59,042
Otherwise, I'd be
too beautiful to deal with.

1204
01:29:59,125 --> 01:30:00,625
[He chuckles]

1205
01:30:00,708 --> 01:30:02,125
That's true.

1206
01:30:03,083 --> 01:30:04,333
Look at this one.

1207
01:30:05,750 --> 01:30:07,750
[Sister] Damn, they were hot!

1208
01:30:08,458 --> 01:30:10,042
I never saw this one.

1209
01:30:10,708 --> 01:30:12,750
Look at Dad! A total fox.

1210
01:30:28,083 --> 01:30:29,500
What's that?

1211
01:30:31,708 --> 01:30:33,333
A marriage license?

1212
01:30:34,542 --> 01:30:37,125
Didn't Mommy say
that she would never, ever get married?

1213
01:30:37,208 --> 01:30:38,875
They didn't.

1214
01:30:39,458 --> 01:30:41,375
Did a little research, and...

1215
01:30:41,458 --> 01:30:42,625
it's all fake.

1216
01:30:42,708 --> 01:30:43,792
What?

1217
01:30:43,875 --> 01:30:48,083
I think they must have needed it
for something involving the authorities.

1218
01:30:51,083 --> 01:30:52,708
[Sister] It sure looks real.

1219
01:30:52,792 --> 01:30:53,958
[Brother] It's not.

1220
01:30:54,542 --> 01:30:56,542
She fabricated the whole thing.

1221
01:31:00,458 --> 01:31:03,417
Well, maybe them
not getting officially married

1222
01:31:03,500 --> 01:31:05,583
is what kept them together
in the first place.

1223
01:31:05,667 --> 01:31:07,042
[Brother] Maybe. Yeah.

1224
01:31:07,125 --> 01:31:08,875
I'd like to think that maybe, you know,

1225
01:31:08,958 --> 01:31:11,208
their deep love
had something to do with it.

1226
01:31:11,292 --> 01:31:12,583
[Sister] You think?

1227
01:31:17,208 --> 01:31:18,625
[Brother] Check these out.

1228
01:31:20,333 --> 01:31:22,167
[Sister] Our birth certificates.

1229
01:31:24,375 --> 01:31:26,208
Are these fake, too?

1230
01:31:26,292 --> 01:31:29,250
[Brother] No. I think they're real.

1231
01:31:29,333 --> 01:31:32,833
But didn't Mommy say that
she lost our actual birth certificates?

1232
01:31:32,917 --> 01:31:34,667
Yeah, I know.

1233
01:31:34,750 --> 01:31:37,167
Man, she was problematic.

1234
01:31:39,167 --> 01:31:40,792
Tell me about it.

1235
01:31:42,083 --> 01:31:43,458
Wow.

1236
01:31:43,542 --> 01:31:48,292
So this makes us...
actual, real-life New Yorkers.

1237
01:31:48,375 --> 01:31:49,958
- This is proof.
- [Brother] Yeah.

1238
01:31:51,417 --> 01:31:53,167
We're native New Yorkers.

1239
01:31:53,250 --> 01:31:55,042
And our passports are legit.

1240
01:31:55,625 --> 01:31:56,875
Wow.

1241
01:31:57,917 --> 01:31:59,917
That's so fucking cool.

1242
01:32:01,250 --> 01:32:03,417
Oh... my God!

1243
01:32:03,500 --> 01:32:05,125
[Sister chuckles softly]

1244
01:32:05,833 --> 01:32:07,625
Miss Falci's class.

1245
01:32:07,708 --> 01:32:09,292
- This is you.
- [Brother] That's me.

1246
01:32:09,375 --> 01:32:12,167
[Sister] We argued about the colors
of the hats that we were gonna use.

1247
01:32:12,250 --> 01:32:14,625
[Sister, laughing] I remember.

1248
01:32:14,708 --> 01:32:16,667
[Brother] We do look a lot alike though.

1249
01:32:16,750 --> 01:32:18,917
[Sister] We do.
I think you got the eyes right.

1250
01:32:19,000 --> 01:32:20,333
[Brother] The beady eyes.

1251
01:32:20,417 --> 01:32:24,042
[Sister] And who thinks
of a double-seat bike when you're like,

1252
01:32:24,125 --> 01:32:26,542
how old were you, like 6 or 5?

1253
01:32:27,250 --> 01:32:28,958
We were so innovative.

1254
01:32:30,042 --> 01:32:31,458
Look at this.

1255
01:32:32,583 --> 01:32:34,208
This is crazy.

1256
01:32:38,750 --> 01:32:40,375
[Sister] Dad is from California?

1257
01:32:42,042 --> 01:32:43,708
Look how young he is.

1258
01:32:44,292 --> 01:32:45,583
Wow!

1259
01:32:46,250 --> 01:32:49,000
And Mom is from Ohio.

1260
01:32:49,083 --> 01:32:52,083
Yep. Look. South Carolina.

1261
01:32:52,167 --> 01:32:54,250
Jersey. Texas.

1262
01:32:54,333 --> 01:32:55,875
[Sister] What?

1263
01:32:55,958 --> 01:32:57,542
[Sister laughs]

1264
01:32:58,875 --> 01:33:01,833
And I got to keep
Dad's beat-to-shit Rolex too.

1265
01:33:02,542 --> 01:33:04,167
[Sister] Wasn't this Granddad's?

1266
01:33:04,250 --> 01:33:05,917
[Brother] That's what Dad said, yeah.

1267
01:33:06,000 --> 01:33:08,167
[Sister] I'm surprised
he wasn't wearing it.

1268
01:33:08,250 --> 01:33:11,875
[Brother] Yeah, me too.
But... nope.

1269
01:33:12,375 --> 01:33:13,583
[Sister] It suits you.

1270
01:33:13,667 --> 01:33:16,292
Dependable, cool.

1271
01:33:16,375 --> 01:33:18,292
Little rough around the edges.

1272
01:33:18,375 --> 01:33:19,958
Just like you.

1273
01:33:21,208 --> 01:33:23,208
Wait. I got one more thing.

1274
01:33:23,292 --> 01:33:25,292
You're really, really gonna love this.

1275
01:33:27,708 --> 01:33:29,042
[Sister] Mom.

1276
01:33:33,292 --> 01:33:35,542
Man, she's such a movie star.

1277
01:33:35,625 --> 01:33:37,583
But wait.

1278
01:33:39,208 --> 01:33:41,625
[She gasps] Oh, my God!

1279
01:33:42,250 --> 01:33:43,417
Okay.

1280
01:33:43,500 --> 01:33:44,917
Wait.

1281
01:33:45,625 --> 01:33:47,083
[She clears her throat]

1282
01:33:47,167 --> 01:33:48,208
You can totally rock those.

1283
01:33:48,292 --> 01:33:50,167
- You look sick. You look really sick.
- Yeah?

1284
01:33:50,250 --> 01:33:52,083
- You think so?
- Yeah.

1285
01:33:52,667 --> 01:33:55,042
You look tight.

1286
01:33:55,125 --> 01:33:56,667
Not as good as her.

1287
01:33:56,750 --> 01:33:59,083
- Ahh, debatable.
- [She laughs]

1288
01:34:25,625 --> 01:34:27,292
I love you so much.

1289
01:34:28,958 --> 01:34:30,958
Thanks for being my brother.

1290
01:34:32,083 --> 01:34:34,917
I got you, man.
Love you, too, Boobie.

1291
01:34:35,833 --> 01:34:37,958
We're gonna be okay.

1292
01:34:38,042 --> 01:34:39,167
Duh.

1293
01:34:42,833 --> 01:34:45,250
I got a little box of other
stuff for you at my place.

1294
01:34:47,125 --> 01:34:48,833
Just objects.

1295
01:34:50,292 --> 01:34:52,583
Little things I thought, you know,

1296
01:34:53,250 --> 01:34:54,708
had their mojo in them.

1297
01:34:56,167 --> 01:34:58,000
I kept a box too, but...

1298
01:34:59,167 --> 01:35:01,000
you can have whatever you want.

1299
01:35:05,417 --> 01:35:07,625
Did they keep a lot of our old stuff?

1300
01:35:08,458 --> 01:35:09,667
Oh, yeah.

1301
01:35:11,375 --> 01:35:13,417
Toys, photos, drawings.

1302
01:35:15,417 --> 01:35:18,417
They kept like every drawing
we ever made.

1303
01:35:19,167 --> 01:35:23,375
They even kept the principal's letter
from that one time we got suspended.

1304
01:35:24,583 --> 01:35:26,958
- Wow.
- You'll see.

1305
01:35:27,792 --> 01:35:30,208
It's not like
I threw anything away, its all...

1306
01:35:30,875 --> 01:35:32,875
carefully packed into storage.

1307
01:35:34,500 --> 01:35:37,917
Good luck finding anything though.
It's... mad full.

1308
01:35:40,833 --> 01:35:42,833
What are we gonna do with it all?

1309
01:35:44,000 --> 01:35:45,417
I don't know.

1310
01:35:51,792 --> 01:35:53,792
Do we have to know right now?

1311
01:35:54,667 --> 01:35:57,750
No. Can't really deal with it.

1312
01:35:57,833 --> 01:36:00,292
[Sirens wail in distance]

1313
01:36:02,792 --> 01:36:05,542
[Keys jingle;
Door opens]

1314
01:36:08,250 --> 01:36:10,125
[Brother, in French] Oh, Madame Gautier,

1315
01:36:10,208 --> 01:36:11,625
you startled us.

1316
01:36:11,708 --> 01:36:14,000
What are you two doing here?

1317
01:36:14,083 --> 01:36:15,750
[Brother] Just here for a last visit.

1318
01:36:15,833 --> 01:36:17,292
But this is no longer your apartment.

1319
01:36:17,375 --> 01:36:18,625
Skye just got back.

1320
01:36:18,708 --> 01:36:20,750
We wanted to say goodbye
one last time.

1321
01:36:21,625 --> 01:36:24,500
You know your parents, bless their souls,

1322
01:36:24,583 --> 01:36:27,167
still owed three month's rent.

1323
01:36:27,250 --> 01:36:31,542
As I told you, Billy,
the agency could've seized all they had.

1324
01:36:31,625 --> 01:36:32,875
Yes.

1325
01:36:32,958 --> 01:36:35,042
[In English] She was nice enough
to prevent that,

1326
01:36:35,125 --> 01:36:36,625
and let me move everything out.

1327
01:36:36,708 --> 01:36:38,333
[Madame Gautier] Yes, I did.

1328
01:36:38,417 --> 01:36:41,292
I kept my word with you, Billy.

1329
01:36:41,375 --> 01:36:43,583
I hope I won't regret it.

1330
01:36:44,667 --> 01:36:45,667
Okay.

1331
01:36:46,250 --> 01:36:48,583
[In French] Give me back the keys
when you leave.

1332
01:36:48,667 --> 01:36:50,417
I will. Thank you.

1333
01:36:50,500 --> 01:36:52,625
[Sister] Thank you, Madame Gautier.

1334
01:36:52,708 --> 01:36:54,458
I'm very happy to see you again.

1335
01:36:55,500 --> 01:36:58,333
It's nice to see you, my little Skye.

1336
01:37:00,875 --> 01:37:02,083
[Sister] <i>Ciao.</i>

1337
01:37:04,667 --> 01:37:06,250
[Door closes]

1338
01:37:11,250 --> 01:37:13,125
[Sister] Do you ever think about

1339
01:37:13,208 --> 01:37:14,667
where they died?

1340
01:37:15,792 --> 01:37:17,458
[Brother] Does it matter?

1341
01:37:17,542 --> 01:37:19,042
Where you die?

1342
01:37:19,875 --> 01:37:21,292
[Sister] I don't know.

1343
01:37:25,458 --> 01:37:30,208
Like, do we even know why
they were flying a plane in the Azores?

1344
01:37:32,125 --> 01:37:36,958
[Brother] Do we even know why they were
ever doing anything anywhere?

1345
01:37:38,000 --> 01:37:39,417
I mean...

1346
01:37:48,375 --> 01:37:50,583
[Bells toll in the distance]

1347
01:38:25,542 --> 01:38:27,167
[Metal rattles]

1348
01:38:28,417 --> 01:38:30,000
[Sister gasps]

1349
01:38:31,417 --> 01:38:32,625
[Sister] Oh my God.

1350
01:38:33,167 --> 01:38:34,917
It's Mom's favourite song.

1351
01:38:35,542 --> 01:38:36,750
Oh, yeah.

1352
01:38:37,708 --> 01:38:38,875
Do it.

1353
01:38:46,792 --> 01:38:49,000
[<i>Spooky </i>by Dusty Springfield plays]

1354
01:38:55,583 --> 01:38:57,833
♪ <i>In the cool of the evening</i>

1355
01:38:57,917 --> 01:39:02,083
♪ <i>When everything
Is gettin' kind of groovy</i>

1356
01:39:04,833 --> 01:39:06,833
♪ <i>You call me up and ask me</i>

1357
01:39:06,917 --> 01:39:11,000
♪ <i>Would I like to go
With you and see a movie</i>

1358
01:39:14,292 --> 01:39:15,667
♪ <i>First I say no</i>

1359
01:39:15,750 --> 01:39:18,875
♪ <i>I've got some plans
For tonight and then I stop</i>

1360
01:39:20,875 --> 01:39:22,833
♪ <i>And say alright</i>

1361
01:39:22,917 --> 01:39:24,875
♪ <i>Love is kind of crazy</i>

1362
01:39:24,958 --> 01:39:28,250
♪ <i>With a spooky little boy like you</i>

1363
01:39:32,125 --> 01:39:33,917
♪ <i>You always keep me guessing</i>

1364
01:39:34,000 --> 01:39:38,625
♪ <i>I never seem to know
What you are thinking</i>

1365
01:39:40,625 --> 01:39:42,875
♪ <i>And if a girl looks at you</i>

1366
01:39:42,958 --> 01:39:47,375
♪ <i>It's for sure
Your little eye will be a winking</i>

1367
01:39:50,542 --> 01:39:54,083
♪ <i>I get confused
'Cause I never know where I stand</i>

1368
01:39:54,167 --> 01:39:56,167
♪ <i>And then you smile</i>

1369
01:39:57,042 --> 01:39:59,083
♪ <i>And hold my hand</i>

1370
01:39:59,167 --> 01:40:04,542
♪ <i>Love is kind of crazy
With a spooky little boy like you</i>

1371
01:40:05,833 --> 01:40:08,750
♪ <i>Spooky</i>

1372
01:40:12,958 --> 01:40:14,958
♪ <i>Spooky</i>

1373
01:40:17,125 --> 01:40:19,000
♪ <i>Spooky</i>

1374
01:40:21,750 --> 01:40:23,375
♪ <i>Spooky</i>

1375
01:40:25,833 --> 01:40:27,792
♪ <i>Spooky... </i>♪

1376
01:40:29,292 --> 01:40:31,875
- [Music fades]
- [Car indicator ticks]

1377
01:41:17,667 --> 01:41:19,000
Eighteen.

1378
01:41:29,333 --> 01:41:31,917
[Metal clanks, squeals]

1379
01:41:39,125 --> 01:41:40,542
Damn, Billy.

1380
01:41:43,250 --> 01:41:45,250
You really cleaned them out.

1381
01:41:46,625 --> 01:41:48,042
[Brother] Yeah.

1382
01:41:48,125 --> 01:41:50,625
And this unit goes back way deep too.

1383
01:41:53,375 --> 01:41:56,583
It never really seemed
like they had that much shit.

1384
01:41:57,083 --> 01:42:00,042
But... they really squirreled
a lot of stuff away.

1385
01:42:04,000 --> 01:42:05,750
[She sighs]

1386
01:42:07,375 --> 01:42:09,542
[Sister] Shit really adds up, don't it?

1387
01:42:09,625 --> 01:42:13,083
[Brother] Half of this stuff is still
boxed up from our grandparents.

1388
01:42:15,583 --> 01:42:16,958
[Brother exhales]

1389
01:42:17,042 --> 01:42:19,000
What are we gonna do with it all?

1390
01:42:21,667 --> 01:42:23,083
I don't know.

1391
01:42:25,875 --> 01:42:28,292
[Brother] Do we have
to know right now, though?

1392
01:42:30,000 --> 01:42:31,292
[Sister] No.

1393
01:42:32,708 --> 01:42:34,333
Can't really deal with it.

1394
01:42:49,875 --> 01:42:51,958
[Metal clanks, rattles]

1395
01:43:04,083 --> 01:43:06,458
[Footsteps recede]

1396
01:43:21,250 --> 01:43:23,625
[<i>These Days </i>by Nico plays]

1397
01:43:40,000 --> 01:43:42,833
♪ <i>I've been out walking</i>

1398
01:43:44,708 --> 01:43:48,792
♪ <i>I don't do too much talking these days</i>

1399
01:43:50,958 --> 01:43:54,208
♪ <i>These days</i>

1400
01:43:55,250 --> 01:44:02,417
♪ <i>These days I seem to think a lot
About the things that I forgot to do</i>

1401
01:44:06,083 --> 01:44:11,250
♪ <i>And all the times
I had the chance to</i>

1402
01:44:19,917 --> 01:44:22,958
♪ <i>I stop my rambling</i>

1403
01:44:24,667 --> 01:44:29,708
♪ <i>I don't do too much gambling these days</i>

1404
01:44:30,667 --> 01:44:34,750
♪ <i>These days</i>

1405
01:44:35,458 --> 01:44:42,667
♪ <i>These days I seem to think about
How all the changes came about my way</i>

1406
01:44:45,958 --> 01:44:53,958
♪ <i>And I wonder if I'd see another highway</i>

1407
01:45:05,542 --> 01:45:08,458
♪ <i>If I had a lover</i>

1408
01:45:10,083 --> 01:45:14,792
♪ <i>I don't think I'd risk another
These days</i>

1409
01:45:15,583 --> 01:45:17,958
♪ <i>These days</i>

1410
01:45:21,375 --> 01:45:28,292
♪ <i>And if I seem to be afraid
To live the life that I have made in song</i>

1411
01:45:31,958 --> 01:45:37,417
♪ <i>It's just that
I've been losing so long </i>♪

1412
01:45:41,458 --> 01:45:43,917
[Slow electric guitar joins]

1413
01:46:03,333 --> 01:46:06,542
♪ <i>I'd stop my dreaming</i>

1414
01:46:07,708 --> 01:46:12,750
♪ <i>I won't do too much scheming these days</i>

1415
01:46:13,667 --> 01:46:17,750
♪ <i>These days</i>

1416
01:46:18,458 --> 01:46:25,500
♪ <i>These days I sit on cornerstones
And count the time in quarter tones to ten</i>

1417
01:46:28,833 --> 01:46:33,917
♪ <i>Please don't confront me
With my failures </i>♪

1418
01:46:34,000 --> 01:46:36,333
[Slow electric guitar resumes]

1419
01:46:36,417 --> 01:46:38,750
♪ <i>I've not forgotten them </i>♪

1420
01:46:50,875 --> 01:46:53,875
[Music fades, stops]

1421
01:46:56,958 --> 01:46:59,417
[Dreamy ambient music]

1422
01:47:56,917 --> 01:47:59,375
[Music continues]

1423
01:48:56,917 --> 01:48:59,375
[Music continues]

1424
01:49:18,583 --> 01:49:21,042
[Music concludes]

1425
01:49:22,250 --> 01:49:24,833
[Ruminative ambient music]

1426
01:50:28,833 --> 01:50:31,292
[Music fades]